"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere is an important statement.\" [3092] \"What?\" I asked. \"Who said it? When?\" [3093] \"At the appropriate time, dear.\" [3094] \"Please tell me, Monsieur Poirot,\" the inspector politely returned to the topic. [3095] \"If you draw a circle around number 19, all the people inside it could have killed Mr. Corry. Mrs. Hemm, the Brandt couple, the MacNodon couple, Miss Watrous. But most importantly, some people have already been set up in the scene. Miss Pemerton may have killed him before going out at about 1:35 p.m., and Miss Webb may have been arranged to meet him, killing him before running out of the room to call the police.\" [3096] \"Ah,\" said the detective, \"now you're getting to the specifics.\" [3097] \"Of course,\" said Poirot, sliding his wheelchair, \"you, my dear Colwyn. You were also on the scene. Finding the lower half of the house on the upper half of the street.\" [3098] \"Oh, really?\" I said indignantly, \"What do you mean?\" [3099] \"I mean that I know all there is to know!\" Poirot declared pompously. [3100] \"And I'm the one who's still trying to tell you the whole thing!\" [3101] \"Murderers are usually arrogant,\" Poirot pointed out, \"and may well play games with you so you have a reason to laugh at me.\" [3102] \"If you go on like this, you'll convince me,\" I said. [3103] I began to feel a little uneasy. [3104] Poirot turned to Inspector Hercule. [3105] \"I say to myself, essentially, it's a simple case of murder. The appearance of the clock, the time advanced by an hour, the deliberate discovery of the body, all of this must be put aside. The essential thing is that an ordinary middle-aged man has died and that someone wanted him to die. If we knew who the dead man was, it would suggest who the murderer is. If he was a notorious con artist, we would have to find those he may have cheated; if he was a detective, we would look for those who had committed a crime under his nose; if he was a rich man, we would look among his heirs. But if we don't know who the man was, finding a suspect from this vast pool is a very daunting task.\" [3106] \"Without considering Miss Pemukh and Miss Hila Webber, who should not have been there, the answer is disappointing. I think only Mr. Lymes has something strange?\" Poirot looked at me inquiringly, and I nodded. \"Everyone has a reason to be trusted. Brand is a famous local architect, MacNorton is a professor at Cambridge University, Mrs. Hamm is the widow of a local auctioneer, and the Waterhouse siblings are respected locals. So let's go back to Mr. Curry. Where does he come from? What brought him to 19 Wilbraham Crescent? The neighbor, Mrs. Hamm, said something very revealing when she found out that the dead man was not living at number 19. 'Oh! I see. He came here to die. How strange.' She has a talent for seeing the essence of things, a talent that only those who are only concerned with their own actions and do not care about others possess. She summed up the whole case. Mr. Curry came to 19 Wilbraham Crescent to die. That's all there is to it!\" [3107] \"Her words suddenly startled me,\" I said. [3108] Poirot did not notice me. [3109] \" 'Dear, dear, dear, come to die.' Mr. Curry came and was killed. But that's not all. His identity cannot be confirmed, which is important. He had no wallet, no identification, and the brand name on his clothes had been torn off. But that's not enough. The card of an insurance agent named Curry was just a temporary measure. If the man's identity cannot be confirmed, he will eventually be given a false identity. It could be his brother, his sister, or his wife. As for the wife, Mrs. Rivale, just the name may have already aroused suspicion. There is a village called Curry-Rivale in Somerset, where I and a friend live nearby \u2013 I don't know what the two names suggest, Mr. Curry, Mrs. Rivale.\" [3110] \"At this point, the plan is almost clear, but what confuses me is why our killer assumes that the police will never be able to determine the actual identity of the deceased. If the man had no family, at least there would be a butler, a servant, or a business partner. This leads me to another assumption: no one knows that the man has disappeared. Another assumption is that he is not British, but only a traveler in this country. This fits with the fact that he had traces of having his teeth treated, but there was no record of any treatment.\" [3111] \"My mind began to form a vague picture of the victim and the killer. The case was carefully planned and skillfully executed, but now there is a flaw that the killer did not anticipate.\" [3112] \"What?\" Hercule asked. [3113] To my surprise, at this point Poirot tilted his head back dramatically and recited a poem:\n[3114] A nail lost, a shoe lost,\n[3115] A shoe lost, a horse lost,\n[3116] One war horse was lost, one war was lost,\n[3117] One war was lost, one empire was lost,\n[3118] All because one horseshoe nail was missing. [3119] He leaned forward. [3120] \"There may be many suspects for the murder of Mr. Curry. But there is only one who could have killed, or had a reason to kill, the girl Iona.\" [3121] We all turned to him at the same time. [3122] \"Let's consider Cavenish Typewriting Agency. There are eight girls working there. On September 9th, four of them were out on an errand, so they wouldn't be back until after lunch. Normally, they would have lunch between 12:30 and 1:30 p.m. The other four, Hilary Webb, Ingrid Brent, and two others, would have lunch between 1:30 and 2:30 p.m. But on that day, Ingrid Brent had a small accident on her way back to the office. Her shoe heel got stuck in the grating, making it difficult for her to walk. So she bought some bread and went straight back to the office.\" [3123] Poirot wagged his strong finger at us. [3124] \"We know that Ingrid Brent was worried about something. She tried to meet Hilary Webb outside the office, but failed. Could this be related to Hilary Webb? We couldn't find any evidence. She might have just wanted to ask Hilary Webb about something that was bothering her. But there was one thing that was clear. She wanted to talk to Hilary Webb outside the office.\" [3125] \"Her statement to the police, that morning, was the only clue we had to what was bothering her. She said, 'I don't know if what she said is true.' Three women testified that day. Ingrid might have been referring to Miss Pemshaw. Or, as we had previously surmised, she might have been referring to Hilary Webb. But there was a third possibility. She might have been referring to Miss Martin.\" [3126] \"Miss Martin? But her testimony only lasted a few minutes.\" [3127] \"Indeed, she only stated the phone call she received claiming it was from Miss Pemerton.\" [3128] \"Are you saying that Ina knew that the phone call was not from Miss Pemerton?\" [3129] \"My inference is even simpler. I believe there was no such phone call at all.\" [3130] He continued speaking:\n[3131] \"Ina's heel broke off. The grille was close to the office. So she returned to the typing pool quickly. But Miss Martindeer was in her own office, unaware that Ina had returned. As far as she was concerned, there was only herself in the office at that time. All she had to do was say that a call came in at 1:49. Ina did not know the severity of the situation at first. Hill went to see Miss Martindeer, who was told there was an appointment to go out. The nature and time of the appointment, Ina did not know. [3132] \"Then the news of the murder broke, and the story unfolded little by little. Miss Pemarho called and asked Hill to come to her. But Miss Pemarho said she had not made the call. The call was said to have come at 2:50 p.m. But Ina knew it was not true. There was no call at that time. Miss Martindeer must have been mistaken, but Miss Martindeer was sure she was not mistaken. The more Ina thought about it, the more puzzled she became. She had to ask Hill. Hill might know. [3133] \"Then the interrogation began. All the girls attended. Miss Martindeer repeated her statement about the call, but Ina knew that the clear evidence and precise time she provided were false. So she went to ask the officer to speak to the chief inspector. I think it's likely that Miss Martindeer overheard her question as she left with the crowd. Or perhaps she heard the girls talking about Ina breaking her heel at that moment, realizing the truth. Anyway, she followed the girl to Wilbraham Crescent. I wonder why Ina went there? [3134] \"Just to see the scene of the murder, I guess,\" Hakaste sighed. [3135] \"Yes, that's right. Maybe Miss Martindeer was talking to her, and they were walking down the street together when Ina casually expressed her doubts. Miss Martindeer decided to take immediate action. They happened to be walking past a phone booth. She said, 'This is very important. You must call the police immediately. Tell them we are going to see them right now.' Following someone's instructions was a habit Ina had formed. She went inside, picked up the phone, and Miss Martindeer followed her, standing behind her, tightening her scarf around her neck, and choking her to death.\" [3136] \"Did nobody see?\" [3137] Poirot shrugged. [3138] \"It should have been someone who saw! But it was exactly 1:00. Lunchtime. And people on New Moon Street were busy looking at this and that. This gave the bold and shameless person the opportunity to act.\" [3139] Hercule Poirot shook his head, incredulous. [3140] \"Miss Martin? I don't understand how she got involved in this case?\" [3141] \"Indeed, it was not immediately apparent. But there is no doubt that Miss Marple killed Ingrid. Oh, yes. Only she could have killed Ingrid, so she must be involved. From Miss Marple, I began to suspect that this murder was a Macbethian[1] one, a cruel and unimaginative boring woman.\u201d\n[3142] \"Lacking imagination?\" Hercule Poirot asked. [3143] \"Oh, yes, very lacking. But very efficient. A first-rate conspirator.\u201d\n[3144] \"But the motive? What was her reason for doing it?\" [3145] Hercule Poirot looks at me. He wags a finger. [3146] \"So, your neighbors' gossip was of no use to you, was it? I found a statement that intrigued me. Do you remember when talking about living abroad, Mrs. Brand said she liked living in Crofton because she had an aunt there. But Mrs. Brand actually had no aunt. She inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada, who was her only surviving relative in the family.\" [3147] Hercule Poirot sat up alertly. [3148] \"So you think \u2013\"\n[3149] Poirot leaned back in his chair, folding his fingers together. He half-closed his eyes, as if dreaming. [3150] \"For example, you are a man, a very ordinary, cautious man, with financial difficulties. One day, you receive a letter from a law firm saying that your wife has inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada. The letter was addressed to Mrs. Brand, but the problem is that the Mrs. Brand who received the letter was not the one mentioned in the letter. She was the second wife, not the first. Imagine how sad and frustrating that would be! But suddenly, there's a solution. Who would know that this was not Mrs. Brand? In Crofton, no one knew that Mr. Brand had been married before. His first marriage was many years ago, during the war, when he was overseas. His wife died soon after they married, and he remarried quickly. He had the original marriage certificate, various other documents, and photos of all the relatives in Canada. Everything was in order. Anyway, it was worth taking a risk. They tried, and succeeded. They went through all the legal procedures. The Brand couple became rich, and their financial difficulties ended \u2013\"\n[3151] \"However, a year later, something unexpected happened to them. What happened? I assume that someone from Canada came to this country and knew all the details about the first Mrs. Brand, and the truth was about to come out. He could be an elderly family executor or a close friend of the family, but whoever he is, he would know. Perhaps they thought they could avoid meeting. Mrs. Brand could pretend to be sick, or she could go abroad, but any similar thing would arouse suspicion. The visitor would insist on seeing the woman he had come to visit specially.\" [3152] \"So, they decided to murder him?\" [3153] \"Yes. Here, I think Mrs. Brand's sister is the mastermind behind the plot.\" [3154] \"You mean Miss Martin and Mrs. Brand are sisters?\" [3155] \"Only then does the story make sense.\" [3156] \"When I saw Mrs. Brand, she reminded me of someone,\" said Hercule Poirot, \"although their personalities were very different, but upon careful reflection, they were somewhat alike. But how could they hope to escape? Someone is missing. They would face interrogation \u2013\"\n[3157] \"If this person went abroad, perhaps just for a vacation, not for business, his schedule would not be fixed. Receiving letters from one place, then postcards from another. By the time people start to suspect, a long time may have passed. Who would associate the already-identified Harry Castleton with the wealthy tourist from Canada who never even set foot in the country? If I were the killer, I would go to France or Belgium for a carefree day of sightseeing, and then deliberately throw the dead man's passport in a train or a car, so that the interrogation could take place there.\" [3158] I unconsciously moved, and Poirot stared at me intently. [3159] \"Right?\" he said. [3160] \"Brand told me he had gone to Bournemouth for a day trip with a blonde woman, and I thought \u2013\"\n[3161] \"That's perfectly normal. Don't doubt it, it's his old habit.\" [3162] \"But these are just conjectures,\" Hercule countered. [3163] \"But you can do your own investigation,\" Hercule said. [3164] He picked up a hotel stationery sheet from the shelf in front of him and handed it to Hercule. [3165] \"You can write to Mr. Endebie at 10 Innisfree Gardens, SW7, who has promised to investigate for me in Canada. He is a well-known international lawyer.\" [3166] \"What about those clocks then?\" [3167] \"Oh! Those clocks! Those remarkable clocks!\" Poirot laughed. \"I think you will find that Miss Rosemary will be held responsible for them. Hence this case, as I said, is quite simple, only made to look fantastic. That clock that Miss Hyde took to be repaired. Did she leave it at the typewriter repair shop? Miss Marple took advantage of this to base her crazy talk on it, perhaps because of that clock, she chose Miss Hyde to be the one who discovered the body\u2014?\" [3168] Hercule suddenly exclaimed loudly:\n[3169] \"You still say this woman is boring and lacks imagination? When did she start planning all this?\" [3170] \"But these were not her plans. That's what makes it interesting. Everything was here, waiting for her. From the very beginning, I discovered this modus operandi, which I was familiar with because I had been reading about it. Very lucky. As Colin will tell you, this week I attended an auction of author's manuscripts. There were some works by Gary Greggson, and I didn't hold out much hope. But luck was on my side. Here\u2014\" Suddenly, he pulled out two old notebooks from the drawer of the table. \"\u2014it's all here! All the plots he planned to write in these books. He died before he could finish them. But Miss Marple, as his secretary, knew all the plots. She could use them to serve her purposes if she wanted to.\" [3171] \"But these clocks must have meant something \u2013 in Mr. Greggson's story, I think.\" [3172] \"Yes, that's right. His clocks were set at five minutes and four seconds, five minutes and seven seconds, and five minutes and five seconds. Together, they form a safe's password, 514547. The safe was hidden behind a replica of the Mona Lisa painting. Inside the safe, \"Poirot continued grumpily, \"was the Russian royal crown. And that's how everything was explained! It was Miss Marple's plot. An innocent girl being framed. Oh, yes, it was easy for Miss Marple. She just had to choose some local characters and make them act according to the plot. So these clear clues led you nowhere! Yes, she is a clever woman. I wonder if Mr. Greggson left her any inheritance? How did he die and what caused his death? I'm curious.\" [3173] Hercule didn't want to hear any more about the past. He put away the notebook and took the hotel business card from my hand. Staring at it, I realized how foolish I had been. [3174] \"Thank you, Poirot, sir,\" Hercule said. \"Your words have given us a lot of inspiration.\" [3175] \"The End\"\n[3176] \"If I have been of any help, I would be very happy.\" [3177] Polo appeared humble. [3178] \"I need to check all the facts -\"\n[3179] \"Of course, of course -\"\n[3180] After bidding farewell, Hercast left. [3181] Poirot turned to me. He frowned. [3182] \"Cheer up. I want to ask you, what's wrong? You look so gloomy.\" [3183] \"I realized how stupid I've been.\" [3184] \"Ah, that's alright. Many people are like that.\" [3185] \"But not you, Hercule Poirot!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAs she reached into her pocket for her keys, a voice came from below, peering out from the corner near the door. [2848] \"A gentleman is waiting for you upstairs.\" [2849] \"Waiting for me?\" [2850] Mrs. Rivard sounded a bit surprised. [2851] \"Well, he can be called a gentleman. He's dressed well and looks good, but not Sir Algernon Phyllotaxis de Phyllotaxis, I'd say.\" [2852] Mrs. Rival finally found the keyhole and inserted it, turning it and going inside. [2853] The house smelled of a mixture of cabbage, fish, and eucalyptus. The smell of eucalyptus seemed to persist throughout the hall. Mrs. Rival held onto the railing and climbed upstairs. She pushed open the door to the first floor and walked in, then suddenly stopped and took a step back. [2854] \"Oh,\" she said, \"it's you.\" [2855] Detective Hakast stood up from his chair. [2856] \"Good evening, Mrs. Rilvall.\" [2857] \"What are you doing here?\" Mrs. Rilvall asked bluntly, unlike her usual manner. [2858] \"Well, I had to come to London for work,\" Detective Hakast said. \"I have a couple of things I'd like to discuss with you, so I came hoping to find you. That woman downstairs thought you'd be back soon.\" [2859] \"Oh,\" Mrs. Rilvall said, \"I don't understand, hmm.\" [2860] Detective Hakast pushed a chair forward. [2861] \"Please sit,\" he said politely. [2862] Their roles seemed reversed, with him as the host and her as the guest. Mrs. Rilvale sat, staring straight at him. [2863] \"What do you mean by a couple of things?\" she asked. [2864] \"Just small matters,\" Detective Chief Inspector Hakast said. \"Sudden small matters.\" [2865] \"You mean, about Harry?\" [2866] \"Exactly.\" [2867] \"Now look here,\" Mrs. Rival said in a challenging tone. A strong smell of liquor hit Detective Hakast's nose. \"My business with Harry is over. I don't want to talk about him. When I saw his picture in the paper, I went straight over and told you about him, didn't I? That was a long time ago, and I don't want to think about it anymore. I can't tell you any more. I've told you everything I can remember, and I don't want to hear any more about it.\" [2868] \"It's just a small detail,\" Detective Hakast said apologetically. [2869] \"Oh, well,\" Mrs. Rival said rudely. \"What is it? Start.\" [2870] \"You think that person is your husband or the person you had a marriage with fifteen years ago, right?\" [2871] \"By now, you should know the exact date, shouldn't you?\" [2872] \"More precise than I thought,\" Chief Inspector Hakakste said to himself, and continued. [2873] \"Yes, you're right. We checked. You got married on May 15, 1948.\" [2874] \"It's always unlucky to be a May bride, people say,\" Mrs. Rival said, sounding dejected. \"It didn't bring me any luck.\" [2875] \"Despite the time that's passed, you can still easily recognize your husband.\" [2876] Mrs. Rivard shifted uncomfortably. [2877] \"He doesn't look old.\" She said, \"Harry always took good care of himself.\" [2878] \"You can give us some additional confirmation information. You wrote to us about that scar, I think.\" [2879] \"Yes. It's behind his left ear. Here.\" Mrs. Rivard raised a hand and pointed to the spot. [2880] \"Behind his 'left ear'?\" Detective Hakast emphasized. [2881] \"Well\u2014\" she seemed to hesitate for a moment. \"Yes. I'm sure. Of course, sometimes left and right can get mixed up, can't they? But yes, on the left side of his neck. Here.\" She indicated the same spot with her hand again. [2882] \"You mean, when he shaved?\" [2883] \"Yes. The dog jumped up at him. We had a very lively dog. It would always jump at you. It was a very affectionate dog. It jumped at Harry, and he had a razor in his hand, and he cut deep into it. He bled a lot. The wound was sewed up, but the scar stayed there.\" [2884] \"That's a very valuable detail, Mrs. Rival. After all, sometimes people can look very much alike, especially after a long time has passed. But it's hard to find a man with the same scar as your husband in the same place. This discovery is quite trustworthy, isn't it? The case seems to have moved forward.\" [2885] \"I'm glad to have satisfied you,\" Mrs. Rival said. [2886] \"When did this razor accident happen?\" [2887] Mrs. Rival paused for a moment. [2888] \"It happened, uh, about six months after we got married. Yes, that's when it was. I remember that's when we started getting that dog.\" [2889] \"So, it happened in October or November of 1948, right?\" [2890] \"Yes.\" [2891] \"After your husband left you in 1951...\"\n[2892] \"I sent him away, not the other way around.\" Mrs. Rivard said with pride. [2893] \"I see. Anyway, since your husband left you in 1951, you haven't seen him until you saw his picture in the newspaper?\" [2894] \"Yes. I've already told you all of this.\" [2895] \"You're quite sure about that, Mrs. Rivard?\" [2896] \"Of course I am. I haven't seen Harry Castleman since that day until I saw the news of his death.\" [2897] \"That is quite strange, you know,\" Detective Hardcastle said. \"That is very strange.\" [2898] \"Why? What do you mean?\" [2899] \"Well, it's a rather odd thing. Scar tissue. Of course, it doesn't matter to us. But doctors will tell you a lot. They'll roughly tell you, you know, how long this scar has been on a man.\" [2900] \"I don't understand what you mean.\" [2901] \"Well, Mrs. Rivale, according to our forensic examination and the opinions of other doctors we've consulted, this scar tissue clearly shows that the wound couldn't have existed for more than five or six years.\" [2902] \"Nonsense,\" said Mrs. Rivale. \"I don't believe it. I... no one can be sure. Anyway, this isn't...\"\n[2903] \"So you see,\" Detective Hakast continued calmly, \"if this scar is only five or six years old, then the man isn't your husband, is he? He left you in 1951, and he didn't have this scar then.\" [2904] \"Maybe he didn't. But he's still Harry, anyway.\" [2905] \"But you haven't seen him since then, Mrs. Rivale. So, since you haven't seen him, how do you know he had this scar five or six years ago?\" [2906] \"You're confusing me,\" Mrs. Rival said. \"You're making me a little confused. Maybe it hasn't been as long as 1948. You can't remember everything. Anyway, I know Harry has that scar.\" [2907] \"I know,\" Detective Hakaste said, standing up. \"I think you should think carefully about what you're saying, Mrs. Rival. You don't want to get into trouble, do you?\" [2908] \"What do you mean, get into trouble?\" [2909] \"Well,\" Detective Hakaste almost apologized, \"perjury.\" [2910] \"Perjury? Me?\" [2911] \"Yes. It's a serious offense, you know. You might get into a dispute, even go to jail. Of course, you didn't swear in the inquest, but you might have to swear in other hearings about your testimony. So, um, I think you should think carefully about it, Mrs. Rivard. Maybe someone is telling you to tell us about this scar.\" [2912] Mrs. Rivard stood up. She straightened her body, and her eyes shone. In that moment, she looked almost dignified. [2913] \"I've never heard such nonsense in my life,\" she said. \"It's all made up. I'm just doing my duty as a citizen. I came to see you, to help you, to tell you everything I remember. If I made a mistake, I'm sure it's natural. After all, I know many, um, gentlemen friends, and sometimes I might get things mixed up. But I don't think I 'did' anything wrong. That man is Harry, and he has a scar on the back of his left ear, and I'm quite sure of it. Now, perhaps, Inspector Hakaste, you should leave, instead of staying here insinuating that I've been lying all along.\" [2914] Inspector Hakaste got up immediately. [2915] \"Goodnight, Mrs. Rivard,\" he said. \"You need to think it over. That's all.\" [2916] Mrs. Rival raised her head. Detective Hakast left through the door. As soon as he was gone, Mrs. Rival's demeanor changed. Her defiant posture collapsed. She looked both fearful and anxious. [2917] \"Give me trouble,\" she murmured. \"Give me trouble. I don't want to do this anymore. I want to, I want to, I don't want to get into trouble for anyone. Tell me lots of things, lie to me, deceive me. Disgusting, too disgusting.\" [2918] She paced back and forth, finally making up her mind. She picked up an umbrella from the corner and went out again. She walked to the end of the street, hesitating in front of a phone booth, and then went to a post office. She went inside, exchanged some coins, and then entered one of the phone booths. She dialed an inquiry number, requesting a transfer to a certain number. She stood there waiting until the call was connected. [2919] \"Connected, please speak.\" [2920] She spoke up. [2921] \"Hello... Oh, it's you. I'm Flo. No, I know you told me not to, but I have to. You never told me it would cause trouble. You just said that if this man was confirmed, it would be bad for you. I never thought I'd be caught up in a murder case... well, you'd say that, but anyway, you never told me... yes. I know. I think you're involved in this mess... well, I won't support it... it will become a \u2014 well, you know what I mean \u2014 an accessory, something like that. Anyway, it's against the facts, and I'm a little scared, I tell you... to write a letter, to tell them about the scar. Now this scar seems to be about four or five years old, and I swear it was before he left... that's perjury, and I might go to jail. Well, your persuasion doesn't help now... no... keeping a promise is one thing... I know... I know you gave me money for this, but not much... well, okay, I'll listen to you, but I won't go... okay, okay, I'll keep it secret... what did you say?... how much?... that's a lot. How am I supposed to know you've already gotten it, even if... well, yes, of course, this will have an impact. Did you swear you had nothing to do with it? \u2014 I mean, killing... not, I believe you won't. Of course, I understand... sometimes mistakes happen because there are too many people... but it's not your fault... you always make things sound so convincing... you always do... well, okay, I'll think about it, but I have to act quickly... tomorrow? What time?... yes... yes, I'll come, but no check. Maybe it will be canceled... I really don't know how I got into this trouble... okay. Well, if you say so... well, I don't mean to be mean... so, okay.\" [2922] She leaves the post office, walking unsteadily along the sidewalk, smiling to herself. [2923] For the sake of that money, it's worth dealing with the police, taking a risk. It will make her life much better afterwards. Besides, it's not really much of a risk. She can say she forgot or didn't remember. Many women don't even remember what happened a year ago. She can say she mixed Harry with another man. Oh, she could come up with many excuses. Mrs. Rival is a naturally fickle person. Her mind is now as active as it was when she was incredibly depressed. She begins to seriously consider the few things she wants to do with the money...\n[2924] \u2014\n[2925] Mrs. Rival is a naturally fickle person. Her mind is now as active as it was when she was incredibly depressed. She begins to seriously consider the few things she wants to do with the money...\n[2926] Chapter 27\n[2927] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[2928] 1\n[2929] \"You don't seem to have learned anything more from Mrs. Lyme.\u201d Colonel Baker complained. [2930] \"There wasn't much to learn.\" [2931] \"You're sure?\" [2932] \"Yes.\" [2933] \"She wasn't one of them?\" [2934] \"No.\" [2935] Colonel Beker gave me a meaningful look. \"Satisfied?\" he asked. [2936] \"Not really.\" [2937] \"Want to know more?\" [2938] \"The truth hasn't come out yet.\" [2939] \"Hmm, we should check out other places... Give up on New Moon Street. Right?\" [2940] \"Yes.\" \"You don't want to talk. Got drunk yesterday?\" [2941] \"I haven't done a good job.\" [2942] \"Want me to pat your head and say, 'There, there'?\" [2943] I couldn't help but chuckle. [2944] \"That's better,\" said Baker. \"Now, what's going on? Is it because of that girl?\" [2945] I shook my head. \"I've been feeling down for a while.\" [2946] \"I've noticed,\" Baker said unexpectedly. \"The world is in chaos now. Problems aren't as clear as they used to be. Once you feel defeated, it's like you've dried up. Be careful of those poisonous mushrooms growing over the wall! If they get the upper hand, your value to us ends. You've done some good work, kid. You should be satisfied with that and go back to your seaweed.\" [2947] He paused and said, \"Do you really 'like' those awful things, right?\" [2948] \"I find the topic interesting.\" [2949] \"I think it should be disgusting. It's an extraordinary mutation, isn't it? I mean, taste. How's the murder case you're handling going? I bet it's that girl who did it.\" [2950] \"You're wrong,\" I said. [2951] Beck waved his finger at me, with a stern yet affectionate expression. [2952] \"I told you to be prepared. I don't see this as a matter for the Boy Scouts.\" [2953] I walked down Charing Cross Road, lost in deep thought. [2954] I bought a newspaper at the subway station. [2955] I read that yesterday, during rush hour, a woman collapsed and lost consciousness at Victoria Station due to exhaustion and was sent to the hospital. Upon arrival at the hospital, it was discovered that she had been stabbed. She did not regain consciousness and died. [2956] Her name was Madame Melina Rival. [2957] 2\n[2958] I called Hakast. [2959] \"Yes,\" he answered my question. \"As the newspaper said.\" [2960] His voice sounded cold and unfeeling. [2961] \"I went to see her the other night. I told her that the scar hadn't been identified yet. The scar tissue had been recently produced through analysis. People make mistakes sometimes. It's just an attempt to do something they shouldn't. Someone paid this woman to confirm that the corpse was her long-abandoned husband. [2962] \"She did it! She thought that if she remembered the insignificant scar later, it would make it more convincing and help with the final confirmation. If she blurted it out immediately, it would sound too casual.\" [2963] \"So, Madame Melina Riviere got caught up in it?\" [2964] \"Do you know, I'm very doubtful about that. Suppose an old friend or acquaintance went to her and said, 'Listen, I'm in a bit of a pickle. A guy I did business with was murdered. If they confirm his identity, all our deals will be exposed, and it will be the end of us. But if you go there and say he's your husband, Harry Castleton, who left you years ago, the whole thing will blow over.'\" [2965] \"Wouldn't she hesitate and refuse to do it, saying it's too risky?\" [2966] \"If so, the person would say, 'What risk? At worst, you'll say you got it wrong. Any woman after fifteen years could possibly have forgotten.\u2019 It's likely that the person mentioned a substantial amount of money at this point. Then she agreed. She played it cool! She went ahead and did it.\" [2967] \"Without any doubt?\" [2968] \"She's not a suspicious woman. Why, oh God, Colin, every time we catch a killer, the people who know him can't believe he would do such a thing!\" [2969] \"What happened when you went to see her?\" [2970] \"I scared her. After I left, she did what I thought she would do - contact the man or woman who got her into this mess. Of course, I followed her. She went to the post office and made a call from a public phone booth. Unfortunately, it wasn't the phone booth I wanted her to use, the one at the end of the street. She had to go get change first. When she came out of the phone booth, she looked happy. She had been under our surveillance, and nothing suspicious had happened until last night. She went to Victoria Station, bought a ticket to Crofton. It was 6:30, rush hour traffic. She wasn't on her guard. She thought, no matter what, she had to see the person in Crofton. But the cunning devil was right in front of her. In the crowded crowd, wanting to gather behind someone and stab them\u2026 it was the easiest thing in the world. Don't imagine she knew she was being stabbed. People don't know, you understand. Remember Barton in the Laverty gang robbery? He walked down the street and fell dead only after a moment of pain. You think you'll be fine in a while. But you won't. You'll die standing up, only you won't know it beforehand.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[847] Chapter Nine\n[848] They drove down Wilbraham Crescent, turned right onto Albany Road, and then turned right again, onto the other side of Wilbraham Crescent. [849] \"That's it,\" Harkness said. [850] \"Once you know,\" Colin said. [851] \"Number 61 is indeed behind Mrs. Hem's house, but it's connected to Number 19 at one corner, which is just great. It will give you a chance to see your Mr. Brand without any outside help.\" [852] \"So that's a beautiful theory,\" the car stopped, and the two men got out. [853] \"Hmm, hmm,\" Colin said, \"look at these gardens!\" [854] This small garden is a perfect example of suburban scenery. There are flower beds with geraniums and African marigolds; lush and plump-leaved begonias; and cute ornaments in the garden - frogs, parasol mushrooms, and funny dwarf statues and fairies. [855] \"I'm sure Mr. Brand is a kind and wealthy man,\" Colin shuddered, \"otherwise he wouldn't have so many unusual ideas. \" When Hakast rang the doorbell, he added, \"Do you think he'll be home at this early hour?\" [856] \"I've called,\" Hakast explained, \"and asked if he's free.\" [857] At this moment, a fashionable small traveling van turned into the garage, obviously a new purchase. Mr. Jotham Brand got out of the car, closed the door, and walked towards them. He was a medium-sized man with a bald head and small blue eyes. He looked friendly and enthusiastic. [858] \"Detective Hakast, please come in,\" he led us into the living room. The furnishings in the room showed the owner's wealth. The room was decorated with expensive and luxurious lamps, a writing desk of the Napoleonic era, a glistening gilded mantelpiece, an inlaid cabinet, and a vase filled with flowers on the windowsill. All the chairs were modern and stylish, with plush cushions. [859] \"Please sit,\" said Mr. Brand warmly, \"smoke? Or do you not smoke when you work?\" [860] \"No, thank you,\" said Hakast. [861] \"Or have a drink?\" Brand said, \"Well, I dare say it's good for both of us. Now, what's the matter? I suppose it's about what happened on number 19? Our garden is connected, but we can't see each other unless we look out the windows upstairs. It seems strange - at least from what I read in the local newspaper this morning. I'm glad to hear from you because it's a good opportunity to get some real news. You can't do anything about all the rumors. It makes my wife anxious, she thinks there's a murderer on the loose. The problem is, how do they let these crazy people out of the mental institution? Is it because they are on parole or for some other reason? Then they go out and do evil to others, and they are sent back to be locked up. As for those rumors! I mean, what our janitor, milkman, and newsboy are gossiping about. You'll be surprised. Some say he was strangled with metal wire, some say he was stabbed, and some say he was beaten to death with a short stick. But in any case, the victim was a man, wasn't it? I mean, not an old woman who was killed? The newspaper reported that it was an unknown man.\" [862] Mr. Brand finally stopped. [863] Hercaster smiled, and said in a nonchalant tone,\n[864] \"Well, about the unknown thing, he left a business card in his pocket with an address.\" [865] \"It seems that the story has added some more information,\" Brand said. \"But you know people. I don't know who made up these things.\" [866] \"Since we're talking about the victim now,\" Hakast said, \"maybe you'd like to see this.\" [867] He took out the police photo again. [868] \"Isn't that him?\" Brand said. \"He looks like an ordinary person, doesn't he? Just like you and me. I don't need to ask if he was killed for a special reason, do I?\" [869] \"It's too early to talk about that,\" Hakast said. \"What I want to know is, Mr. Brand, have you ever seen this man before?\" [870] Brand shook his head. [871] \"I'm sure I haven't. I'm good at remembering faces.\" [872] \"He didn't visit you for any particular reason - selling insurance or vacuum cleaners or washing machines or the like?\" [873] \"No, no. Definitely not.\" [874] \"We should ask your wife,\" Hakast said. \"After all, if he called on you, she would have seen him.\" [875] \"Yes, that's true. I don't know, though... Valerie's not feeling well, you know. I don't want to upset her. I mean, well, this photo of him being murdered, what do you think?\" [876] \"Yes,\" Hakast said. \"That's true. But anyway, it's not a particularly unpleasant picture.\" [877] \"No, not at all. This photo is great. In fact, the guy looks like he's sleeping.\" [878] \"Are you talking about me, Jotham?\" [879] The door to the adjacent room opened, and a middle-aged woman walked in. Hakast thought she had been listening carefully to what was going on in the room next door. [880] \"Oh, there you are, dear,\" Brand said. \"I thought you were napping. This is my wife, Detective Hakast.\" [881] \"That terrible murder,\" Brand's wife murmured. \"I shudder just thinking about it.\" [882] She sighed softly and sat down on the couch. [883] \"Lift your feet, dear,\" Brand said. [884] Brand's wife did as she was told. She was a woman with sandy hair, speaking in a weak voice, pale complexion, and seemed to be enjoying her current state of illness. After a while, she reminded Hakast of someone, but he couldn't remember who. The weak and melancholic voice continued:\n[885] \"My health is not good, Detective Hakast, so my husband naturally lets me avoid things that would frighten or worry me. I'm sensitive. You must be talking about a photo, I think, of the man who was murdered. Oh, dear, that sounds so terrible! I don't know if I can bear to see it!\" [886] \"I really want to see it,\" Hakast thought to himself. [887] He said with a slight malice:\n[888] \"Actually, I think it's best not to show you that photo, Mrs. Brand. I just think you might be able to help us, see if this man ever came to visit you.\" [889] \"I must do my duty, mustn't I?\" Mrs. Brand said, smiling sweetly and bravely. She extended her hand. [890] \"Valerie, have you ever thought this would make you uncomfortable?\" [891] \"Don't be ridiculous, Josea. Of course, I want to see it.\" [892] She looked at the photo with a strong curiosity, and the detective thought the same, but he was disappointed. [893] \"He looks, really, he doesn't look dead at all,\" she said, \"not at all like he was murdered. He was, wasn't he strangled?\" [894] \"He was stabbed to death,\" said the detective. [895] Mrs. Brand closed her eyes and trembled. [896] \"Oh, my goodness,\" she said, \"how terrible.\" [897] \"Have you seen him, Mrs. Brand?\" [898] \"No,\" Mrs. Brand looked reluctant. \"No, I haven't. Is he one of those door-to-door salesmen?\" [899] \"He seems to be an insurance agent,\" the detective said seriously. [900] \"Oh, I see. No, they've never come around here, I'm sure. You don't remember me mentioning anything like that, do you, Josea?\" [901] \"Not at all,\" Mr. Brand said. [902] \"Is there any connection between him and Miss Pembroke?\" Mrs. Brand asked. [903] \"No,\" the detective replied, \"she doesn't know him at all.\" [904] \"Strange,\" Mrs. Brand said. [905] \"Do you know Miss Pembroke?\" [905]\n[906] \"Yes, I mean, because we're neighbors, of course I know her. She used to come and ask my husband for advice on gardening.\" [907] \"You must be a pretty good gardener then,\" said the detective. [908] \"Not really, not really,\" said Mr. Brand dismissively, \"I don't have the time, you know. I just know a little bit, but I do have a good guy who comes twice a week. He takes care of the plants, weeds, and keeps the garden tidy. I can say my garden is one of the best in the neighborhood, but I'm not a real gardener, my neighbor is.\" [909] \"Mrs. Lyme?\" asked Hakastert, surprised. [910] \"No, no, it's Mr. McNaughton from 63. He lives for his garden. He spends all day in the garden, piling on compost. He's a real pain in the neck when it comes to compost, but I don't think that's what you want to talk about, is it?\" [911] \"Maybe not.\" The detective said. \"I was just wondering if anyone, you or your wife, for example, was in the garden yesterday at that time. After all, as you said, you're right next to number 19, maybe you had a chance to see or hear something strange.\" [912] \"At noon, is that the time? I mean, the murder happened at that time, right?\" [913] \"Around one to three o'clock.\" [914] Brand shook his head. \"I didn't see anything at that time. I was here, Valerie was too. We were having lunch together, our dining room window faces the street, we couldn't see anything that happened in the garden.\" [915] \"What time did you have lunch?\" [916] \"Around one. Sometimes one-thirty.\" [917] \"Did you go back to the garden after lunch?\" [918] Brand shook his head. [919] \"In fact,\" he said, \"my wife usually goes upstairs for a rest after lunch, unless there's a lot to do. I take a little nap on that chair over there. I have to leave at, um, I think it's a quarter to three. But unfortunately, I didn't go back to the garden.\" [920] \"Oh, well,\" said Hercule, sighing. \"We'll have to ask everyone.\" [921] \"Of course, of course. I wish I could help more.\" [922] \"This house is really nice,\" the detective said. \"Must have cost a pretty penny.\" [923] Brand smiled cheerfully. [924] \"Well, we like all the nice things. My wife has good taste. We got a windfall a year ago. My wife inherited some money from her uncle. She hadn't seen him in twenty-five years. Quite a surprise, eh? It certainly made a difference for us, letting us live better, and we're planning a trip this year. To places like Greece, I think they're ancient countries. Many scholars have mentioned them in speeches. Well, of course, I'm a self-made man, and I don't have much time to study these things, but I'm interested. The guy who discovered Troy, for example, was said to be a grocer, how romantic. To be honest, I like to go abroad, not like the usual traveling, just occasionally spending a weekend in Paris, that's enough. Sometimes I think about selling this place and going to Spain or Portugal, or even the West Indies, to live there, of course, just dreaming. Many people do that, and it saves a lot of income tax, but my wife doesn't like the idea.\" [925] \"I like to travel too, but I don't want to live anywhere but England.\" Mrs. Brand said. \"We have all our friends here. My sister lives here, and many people know us. If we went abroad, we'd be strangers. And, we have a good doctor here who knows my health condition well. I don't want to see a foreign doctor; I don't have faith in them.\" [926] \"Wait and see,\" said Mr. Brand cheerfully. \"When we go on vacation, you'll fall in love with Greece.\" [927] Mrs. Brand looked unconvinced. [928] \"I think there should be a suitable British doctor on board,\" she said doubtfully. [929] \"Certainly,\" said her husband. [930] He saw Mr. Hakast and Mr. Coling to the door, expressing his regret again for not being able to help them more. [931] \"Well,\" Huckleberry said, \"what do you think of him?\" [932] \"I wouldn't let him build me a house,\" Colly said. \"An unscrupulous little builder isn't the kind of person we want to concern ourselves with. We're looking for someone with a sense of dedication. As for the murder case you're handling, things always seem to go wrong. Now, if Brand had poisoned his wife with arsenic or pushed her into the Aegean Sea, then married a sophisticated blonde \u2013\"\n[933] \"We'll handle it when it happens,\" Detective Huckleberry said. \"For now, we need to continue investigating the murder case.\" [934] Chapter 10\n[935] At 62 Wilbraham Crescent, Mrs. Rymes encouraged herself, \"There are only two days left, only two days.\" She pushed back a few damp strands of hair from her forehead. A tremendous crash came from the kitchen. Mrs. Rymes didn't even want to go over and see what the noise was. If she could just pretend she hadn't heard it. Oh, dear! There are only two days left. She walked through the hall and flung open the kitchen door, the sound much more moderate than three weeks ago,\n[936] \"What have you done?\" [937] \"Sorry, Mom,\" said her son Bill. \"We were using these jars as bowling balls, playing a game, and somehow some of them rolled under the cupboard.\" [938] \"We didn't mean to let them roll under the cupboard,\" said her younger son Ted excitedly. [939] \"Well, pick up these things and put them all back in the cupboard, sweep up the broken ceramics, and throw them in the trash.\" [940] \"Oh, Mom, not now?\" [941] \"Yes, do it now.\" [942] \"Ted will do it,\" said Bill. [943] \"How dare you,\" said Ted, \"always making me do it. If you don't do it, I won't do it either. I bet you'll do it.\" [944] \"I bet I won't do it.\" [945] \"I'll make you do it.\" [946] \"You dare!\" [947] The two children started wrestling fiercely. Ted was pushed hard by Bill, his back hitting the table, and a bowl containing eggs was about to topple over and shake. [948] \"Get out of the kitchen!\" Mrs. Lyman exclaimed. She pushed the children out of the kitchen, closed the door, and began to pick up the jars and sweep up the pieces of broken crockery. [949] \"In two days,\" she thought, \"they'll be back at school! How delightful and happy it will be for a mother.\" [950] She vaguely remembered a witty comment from a column writer:\n[951] On the first and last day of the holidays. How true, Mrs. Rimsay thought, as she swept up the fragments of her beloved dishes. How joyfully she had anticipated their return five weeks ago! But now? \"Tomorrow,\" she repeated, \"Bill and Ted will be back at school. I can hardly believe it. I can't wait!\" [952] How happy they were to see them five weeks ago at the station! Their warm, affectionate greeting! Running around the house and garden. The specially prepared cake for tea. But what did she look forward to now? A quiet day. No more rushing to prepare food, no more constant busyness. She loved these children. They were good children, that was beyond doubt. She was proud of them. But they were also exhausting. Their voracious appetite, their energy, and their noisy voices. [953] Just then, there was a piercing scream. She turned her head alertly. That was it, they had gone to play in the garden. That would be better, there was plenty of space in the garden. But they might annoy the neighbors. She prayed they would stay away from Mrs. Black's cat. Honestly, it wasn't for the cat's sake, but because the wire fence surrounding Mrs. Black's garden was very likely to tear their shorts. She glanced at the first aid kit, which was on the dressing table beside her. [954] This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this silence. She stood indecisively, the basket of broken porcelain still in her hand. The kitchen door opened, and Bill stood there. His eleven-year-old face wore an expression of both fear and ecstasy, unlike anything he usually showed. [955] A sudden, excited scream was cut off, followed by silence. This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this silence. She stood indecisively, the basket of broken porcelain still in her hand. The kitchen door opened, and Bill stood there. His eleven-year-old face wore an expression of both fear and ecstasy, unlike anything he usually showed. [956] \"Mom,\" Bill said, \"a detective has come, and a man is following him.\" [957] \"Oh,\" Mrs. Lymes sighed, \"What does he want, dear?\" [958] \"He wants to see you,\" Bill said, \"but I think it's about the murder that happened at Miss Pembroke's house yesterday.\" [959] \"I don't understand why he would come to me,\" Mrs. Lymes said, a little annoyed. [960] Life is full of one thing after another, she thought. What should she do with the potatoes for the Irish stew? And why did the detective come at such an awkward time? [961] \"Well, alright,\" she said, sighing, \"I suppose I should go.\" [962] She emptied the broken crockery into the trash can under the sink, washed her hands under the faucet, and smoothed her hair, ready to follow Bill out. He was getting impatient. \"Hurry up, Mom.\" [963] Mrs. Limehouse entered the living room, with Bill standing beside her. Two men were there, and her little son Ted was with them, his eyes wide and envious. [964] \"Mrs. Limehouse?\" [965] \"Good morning.\" [966] \"I believe these young men have already told you? I'm Detective Hackett.\" [967] \"It's rather embarrassing,\" Mrs. Lymsey said. \"I'm quite busy this morning. Will this take a long time?\" [968] \"Just a little while,\" Detective Hackett tried to reassure her.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nHe sets a trap, a very obvious trap, so that the person he wants to catch often says, 'This is too obvious. It can't be true.' And then they fall into the trap!\" [1480] I laughed. \"Yes,\" I said. \"It's not fashionable these days for sons to admire their fathers. They seem to sit down and write their memoirs, filled with bitterness and resentment, remembering all the unpleasant things they can remember. But personally, I respect my father. I even hope to be as good as him, of course not by following his every step.\" [1481] \"But it's almost the same,\" Poirot said. \"Almost exactly the same, although you have to work behind the scenes, and he doesn't. He coughed lightly. \"I should congratulate you on your recent remarkable success. The Larkin case, isn't it?\" [1482] \"Things are going well so far,\" I said, \"but I have much more to do. And I didn't come here today to talk about that.\" [1483] \"Of course, of course,\" Poirot said, waving for me to sit in a chair and offering me a cup of herbal tea, which I declined. [1484] George came in at this point, carrying a bottle of whisky, a glass, and a straw, and placed them next to me. [1485] \"What have you been up to lately?\" I asked Poirot. [1486] \"Looking at all these books around you,\" I said, \"it seems you've been doing some research?\" [1487] Poirot sighed. \"You could say that. Yes, in a way, it's true. Recently, I've been eager to find a problem. Any problem will do, I tell myself, like Sherlock Holmes. The depth of parsley in butter. The most important thing is to have a problem. I need to exercise not muscles, you understand, but brain cells.\" [1488] \"Just staying healthy,\" I said. [1489] \"As you said,\" he sighed. \"But, dear, this problem is hard to come by. Last Thursday, someone brought me such a problem. Three dry orange peels appeared on my umbrella stand for no reason. How did they get here? How could they possibly be here? I don't eat oranges myself. George would never put dry orange peels on the umbrella stand. Visiting guests couldn't possibly carry three orange peels. Ah, this is indeed a problem.\" [1490] \"Did you solve this problem?\" [1491] \"Yes,\" Poirot said. [1492] His voice was more melancholy than proud. [1493] \"The final result was not very interesting. The original cleaning lady was replaced by someone new, who brought her child along. This violated the rules. Although this may not sound very interesting, it requires a persistent pursuit to uncover various disguises and lies. This problem was satisfactory but not a big one.\" [1494] \"That's disappointing,\" I said. [1495] \"Well,\" Poirot said, \"I am a modest person. But to be honest, killing a chicken doesn't require a knife.\" [1496] I shook my head seriously. Poirot continued, \"Later, I spent time reading various unsolved mysteries in real life. I applied my own methods to solve these problems.\" [1497] \"Do you mean cases like the Bravo case, the Adelaide Bateley case, and others?\" [1498] \"Exactly. In a way, it's easy. I'm very sure who killed Charles Bravo. There may be others involved, but she's not the key figure in the whole thing. Then there was the unfortunate teenager, Constance Kent. The real motive behind her strangling her beloved younger brother is always hard to fathom. But not for me. I found a clue when I read this case. I was very sure of the answer. Alas, they are probably dead by now.\" [1499] I pondered to myself that humility was not one of Hercule Poirot's strengths. [1500] \"What should I do next?\" Poirot continued. [1501] I suppose he had been talking so much that he was enjoying the sound of his own voice. [1502] \"I turned from real life to novels. You can see these different kinds of crime novels on either side of me. I've been going through history, here \u2014\" He picked up the book on the arm of the chair as I came in. \"Here, my dear Colonel, is 'The Case of the Living Dummy.'\" [1503] \"That was a long time ago,\" I said. \"I remember my father mentioning that he read it when he was a boy. I should have read it too. Now it seems outdated.\" [1504] \"This book is excellent,\" Poirot said. \"You can really feel the atmosphere of the time and the careful plotting and thoughtful narrative. The descriptions of the blond Eleanor and the moonlit Mary are so rich and vivid!\" [1505] \"I must read it again,\" I said. \"I've forgotten the details about those beauties.\" [1506] \"There's a maid named Hannah, very typical, and a murderer, a perfect psychological study.\" [1507] I felt like I was in a lecture. I listened calmly. [1508] \"Now let's talk about 'The Adventures of Arsen\u00e8 Lupin', \" Poirot continued. \"So mythical, so fantastical! But the content presented is so lively, so full of vitality, so vivid! The story is absurd, but it can be flaunted. It's also a kind of humor.\" [1509] He put down 'The Adventures of Arsen\u00e8 Lupin' and picked up another book. \"This is 'The Secret of the Yellow Room'. Ah, this is a true classic! From beginning to end, I've always held this view. Such a skillful logical reasoning! The voices criticizing it, I remember, said it violated the rules, my dear Colin. No, no, maybe a little, but not too much. Just some subtle differences. No. There is a truth that runs through the whole story, but it is wrapped in subtle and clever words. When you reach the intersection of the three corridors, everything will be revealed at that moment.\" He asserted solemnly, \"A true masterpiece, but I have almost forgotten it.\" [1510] Poirot jumped back to the works of late authors twenty years later. [1511] \"I have also read some of Madame Ariadne Oliver's early works,\" he said. \"She is my friend, and I believe she is also your friend. I do not agree with her works, please note. Her works tell stories that are extremely unlikely to happen. The build-up to the climax of the events is too long and unnatural. As a young writer of that time, she clumsily created her detective, a Finn, but it is clear that she knows nothing about the Finns or Finland. She has the habit of being original, and she occasionally creates a profound detective story. In later years, she learned a lot of things she didn't know before. For example, the procedures of the police investigation. The theme of light weapons is also described well now. What she needs most now is to have a lawyer or barrister as a friend to write about the exact content of court proceedings.\" [1512] He put down Madame Ariadne Oliver's works and picked up another book. [1513] \"Now it's Mr. Cyril Quin. Ah, he is a master who provides alibis for the crime scene.\" [1514] \"If I'm not mistaken, he's a very boring writer,\" I said. [1515] \"That's true,\" said Poirot. \"There is nothing particularly shocking in his books. Of course, there will be a corpse, occasionally more. But the plot always revolves around alibis, train schedules, bus routes, and cross-country designs. I admit, I like the intricate and meticulously designed alibis. I like to unravel Mr. Cyril Quin's designs.\" [1516] \"I think you always succeed,\" I said. [1517] Poirot was honest. [1518] \"Not always,\" he admitted. \"No, not always. Of course, you will find that his books are similar after a while. Although the alibi always occurs in different circumstances, the design layout is similar. You know, dear Colin, I imagine Cyril Quinn sitting in his room, smoking his pipe, just like the photo of him, surrounded by the ABC alphabet, the Continental train timetable, airline brochures, and various schedules, even the schedules of the ferry. What do you want to do, Colin, Cyril Quinn always has his way.\" [1519] He put down Cyril Quinn's book and picked up another one. [1520] \"Now it's Mr. Gary Gregson. The author of horror novels, prolific. I know he has written sixty-four of them. He seems to be the opposite of Quinn. In Quinn's works, things always happen slowly; while in Gary Gregson's works, too many things always happen at the same time. The plot is unbelievable and always difficult to follow due to massive chaos. They are all vividly colored. Blood, cottages, corpses, clues, and expanding fear. Everything is terrible, and nothing is real life. As you said, he is not my cup of tea. He is, in fact, not even a cup of tea. He is more like one of those more obscure American cocktails, the ingredients of which are very questionable.\" [1521] Poirot paused, sighed, and continued his speech. \"Now let's talk about American writers. He picked up a book from the pile on his left. \"This is by Florence Eckle, whose works are orderly and methodical, with plenty of action. Yes, everything is there. Rich and varied plots, brisk and lively pacing. She's a sharp thinker, but like many American writers, she seems to have a fondness for liquor. You know, I'm a connoisseur of wine. It's a joy to have a little local and vintage red wine or Burgundy in the story. However, if a detective in an American horror novel drinks a certain amount of black whiskey and bourbon on every page, it becomes boring and tiresome. Whether he drinks a pint or a half-pint, I don't think it affects the story. However, the drinking motive in American books is everywhere and unavoidable.\" [1522] \"How do you view the hardboiled genre?\" I asked. [1523] Poirot waved his hand, as if swatting away an unwanted fly or mosquito. [1524] \"For violence's sake? When did you become interested in this? When I first became a policeman, I witnessed many violent incidents. You may have read a medical textbook. However, overall, I highly rate American crime novels. I think they are more cunning and imaginative than British novels, and less focused on emotions and atmosphere than most French authors. Let's take Louisa O'Malley as an example.\" [1525] He bent down to search for another book. [1526] \"Her work is an excellent example of academic writing, yet readers are often excited and worried by it. Look at these brownstone mansions in New York's exclusive neighborhoods. But what is brownstone? I've never known. Those apartments that prohibit entry, those snobbish behaviors, those hidden crimes, all creeping in. These evils occur, and they have indeed occurred. Louis O'Malley is a remarkable writer, a truly remarkable writer.\" [1527] He sighed, leaned back, and shook his head, finishing the rest of his herbal tea. [1528] \"And then, there's always your favorite.\" [1529] He bent over again, intent on finding a book. [1530] \"The Complete Sherlock Holmes,\" he murmured affectionately, even reverently, \"a master!\" [1531] \"Sherlock Holmes?\" I asked. [1532] \"Ah, no, no, not Sherlock Holmes! I pay tribute to its author, Sir Arthur Conan Doyle. These stories about Sherlock Holmes are unattainable in reality, filled with errors and artificial strategies. But the art of this work, ah, is entirely different. It allows one to fully enjoy the beauty of language, especially the outstanding Dr. Watson. Ah, that was a great success.\" [1533] He sighed and shook his head, muttering thoughtfully, obviously thinking of something:\n[1534] \"My dear Hastings. My friend Hastings, whom I often mention. I haven't heard from him in a long time. He went to South America, where he hid his identity, what a ridiculous thing to do, where there are always revolutions.\" [1535] \"This is not just happening in South America,\" I said. \"Revolution is happening all over the world today.\" [1536] \"Let's not discuss this explosive issue,\" Hercule Poirot said, \"even if we have to discuss it, let's not discuss it.\" [1537] \"Actually,\" I said, \"I came to discuss something completely different.\" [1538] \"Ah! Are you getting married, are you?\" Poirot asked, \"I'm so happy, dear, so happy.\" [1539] \"How did you guess that, Poirot?\" I asked, \"There's nothing like that.\" [1540] \"It happens all the time,\" Poirot said, \"It happens every day.\" [1541] \"Perhaps it does,\" I said firmly. \"But not for me. In fact, I came to tell you that I encountered a very small problem in the murder case.\" [1542] \"Really? A small problem in the murder case? You brought it to me. Why?\" [1543] \"Well...,\" I felt a little embarrassed. \"I thought you would be willing to help.\" [1544] Poirot looked thoughtful as he stroked his mustache with his delicate hand. Then he spoke. [1545] \"Master,\" he said, \"his dog is always very friendly. Outside, he throws a ball for the dog to play with. The dog, of course, is very good to its master. The dog catches rabbits or mice and then brings them to the master's feet. Then what does it do? It wags its tail.\" [1546] I couldn't help but laugh. \"Am I wagging my tail?\" [1547] \"I think so, my friend. Yes, I believe you are.\" [1548] \"Well,\" I said, \"then what did the master say? Did he want to see the dog catch a mouse? Did he want to know all about it?\" [1549] \"Of course, that's natural. Do you think I would be interested in this case?\" [1550] \"Well, mainly,\" I said, \"the case doesn't make sense.\" [1551] \"That's impossible,\" said Poirot. \"Everything has a reason. Everything.\" [1552] \"Well, try then,\" I said. \"I failed. I'm not really involved in this case, just happened to come across it. You know, once the identity of the deceased is established, the whole case is simple.\" [1553] \"You speak without method or logic,\" said Poirot gravely. \"Please give me the facts. You say it's a murder case, do you?\" [1554] \"Yes, it's a murder case,\" I confirmed. \"Well, here's what happened.\" [1555] I detailed to him what had occurred at 19 Wilbraham Crescent. Poirot leaned back in his chair, closing his eyes as he listened, and tapping his index finger gently on the armrest of the chair. When I finally finished, he didn't speak immediately, and for a while, he remained with his eyes closed, asking:\n[1556] \"Not a joke, I hope?\" [1557] \"Oh, absolutely not,\" I said. [1558] \"Remarkable,\" said Hercule Poirot. He savored the word with his tongue, then repeated it syllable by syllable. \"Re-mark-able.\" After that, his fingers continued to tap lightly on the armrest, and he nodded slowly. [1559] \"Hmm,\" I waited impatiently after a while. \"What do you think?\" [1560] \"So, what do you want me to say?\" [1561] \"I want you to tell me the solution. I know from you that if you lean back in your chair and think about the whole event, the answer will come to you. There's no need to ask people or run around looking for clues.\" [1562] \"I always do that.\" [1563] \"Oh, that's just talk,\" I said. \"I've told you the facts, and now I want the answer.\" [1564] \"Just that, is it? But there's more to analyze, my friend. We're only at the beginning of the whole thing, aren't we?\" [1565] \"I still want you to point something out.\" [1566] \"I understand.\" He thought for a moment. \"There is one thing that is certain,\" he asserted. \"This must be a very simple case.\" [1567] \"Simple? I'm surprised.\" [1568] \"Of course.\" [1569] \"Why do you say it must be simple?\" [1570] \"Because it looks so complicated. If it must look complicated, then it must be simple. Can you understand that?\" [1571] \"I don't quite get it.\" [1572] \"Strange,\" Poirot pondered. \"What you just told me, I think, yes, some of the details are familiar. Now I'm trying to remember where and when I encountered these events\u2026\" He stopped. [1573] \"Your memory,\" I said, \"must be a vast repository of crime cases. But you can't remember everything, can you?\" [1574] \"Unfortunately, no,\" Poirot said. \"But these memories can be helpful from time to time. I remember, there was a soap-boiler in Li\u00e8ge [2]. He poisoned his wife to marry a blonde stenographer. It became a typical case. Later, much later, the same thing happened. I noticed it. This time it was a kidnapped poodle, but the method was the same. I found similarities between the case of the blonde stenographer and the soap-boiler, look! That's what it is. Now in the case you just told me, I have the same feeling of d\u00e9j\u00e0 vu.\" [1575] \"Clocks?\" I prompted him hopefully. \"A fake insurance agent?\" [1576] \"No,\" Poirot shook his head. [1577] \"The blind woman?\" [1578] \"No, no, no. Don't disturb me.\" [1579] \"I'm disappointed in you, Poirot,\" I said. \"I thought you would directly tell me the answer.\" [1580] \"But, my friend, what you just told me is just a pattern. There are many things left to be discovered. Assuming the person's identity is determined. The police are very good at this. They have criminal records, they can run ads with the dead person's photo to find people, they can contact the list of missing persons, they can take the dead person's clothes for scientific testing, and so on. Oh, yes, there are hundreds of other methods they can use. Don't doubt it, the person's identity will definitely be found out.\" [1581] \"So there is nothing to do now, is there?\" [1582] \"There will always be something to do.\" Hercule Poirot said gravely. [1583] \"Such as?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[2707] \"Tell me about the people who live in that house,\" I said. \"What did you see? Who lives in that clean house next door?\" [2708] \"Oh, the house next door is where a blind woman lives. She can't see at all, but she walks around like she can. That's what the caretaker, Harry, told me. He's a nice man. He told me about a murder case.\" [2709] \"A murder case?\" I pretended to be surprised. [2710] Geraldine nodded. Her eyes gleamed as she was about to begin telling an important story. [2711] \"The murder happened in that house. I could see it.\" [2712] \"How interesting.\" [2713] \"Isn't it? I've never seen a murder before. I mean, I've never seen a place where a murder happened.\" [2714] \"What did you see, um, what?\" [2715] \"Well, at first nothing happened. You know, it was just an ordinary day. The exciting part happened when I heard a scream, and someone came running out of the house. Then I knew something had happened.\" [2716] \"Who was screaming?\" [2717] \"It was a woman. She was very young and very beautiful. She ran out of the door and kept screaming. A young man walked up the street towards her. She ran out of the gate and grabbed him tightly, like this.\" She demonstrated with her arms. She suddenly fixed her gaze on me. \"He looked just like you.\" [2718] \"Well, I must have some sort of cloning power.\" I said sarcastically. \"What happened next? It's so thrilling.\" [2719] \"Well, he made her sit down. You know, on the ground. Then he went into that house, the Emperor\u2014that's the orange cat I call Emperor because it looks so proud\u2014stopped washing itself and looked very surprised. Then Miss Paxtaville came out of her house. That's the one, number 18. She came out and stood on the steps looking around.\" [2720] \"Miss Paxtaville?\" [2721] \"I call her Miss Paxtawf because she looks so ordinary. She has a brother, and she often bullies him.\" [2722] \"Go on.\" I said with interest. [2723] \"Then all sorts of things happened. The man ran out of the house again. Are you sure it wasn't you?\" [2724] \"I'm very ordinary-looking,\" I said modestly. \"A lot of people look like me.\" [2725] \"Yes, I suppose so.\" Geraldine looked frank. \"Well, anyway, this man, he walked down the street, then made a call from a phone booth on the side of the road. The police came soon. Many police. They carried the dead person into what looked like an ambulance car. Of course, there were a lot of people, rushing to see what was happening. I saw Harry. He's the caretaker of these apartments. It was he who told me about it.\" [2726] \"Did he tell you who was murdered?\" [2727] \"He just said it was a man. Nobody knows his name.\" [2728] \"That's quite interesting,\" I said. [2729] I sincerely prayed that Ingrid wouldn't come in with a delicious syrup-filled pastry or some other delicacy at this moment. [2730] \"Think back to what happened before that. Tell me something earlier. Have you seen this person? The one who was murdered. Did you see him come to that house?\" [2731] \"No, I didn't. I think he was there all along.\" [2732] \"Do you mean he lived there?\" [2733] \"Oh, no, no one lives there except Miss Pemukh.\" [2734] \"So you know her real name?\" [2735] \"Well, yes, it was in the paper. About the murder case. The screaming girl's name is Sheila Weber. Harry told me the name of the man who was murdered is Mr. Corry. That's an interesting name, isn't it, like some food brand. And there was another murder case. Not on the same day, later, at a phone booth on the side of the road. I could see it from here, but I had to stick my head out the window and twist my neck to look. Of course, I didn't really see because, you know, if I had known there was a case, I would have paid attention to look. But, of course, I can't be a fortune teller. There were a lot of people gathered there that morning, just standing on the street, looking at the house across the way. I think they were really stupid, don't you?\" [2736] \"Yes,\" I said, \"absolutely stupid.\" [2737] Ingledew appeared again. [2738] \"I'll be right back,\" she reassured us. \"I'll be right back.\" [2739] She left again. Geraldine said:\n[2740] \"We really don't need her. She just worries about cooking. Well, that's the only meal she makes besides breakfast. Dad eats in the dining room, and he brings me something from there. It's usually fish or something else, not a proper dinner.\" [2741] \"What time do you usually have lunch, Geraldine?\" [2742] \"My main meal, you mean? That's my main meal. I don't have dinner, just a snack. Well, I usually eat my main meal when Ingrid is done cooking. She has no sense of time. She's pretty punctual for breakfast, or Dad gets mad, but lunch is whenever. Sometimes we eat at twelve, sometimes not until two. Ingrid says you don't have to wait for a set time to eat, you just eat when it's ready.\" [2743] \"Well, that's a rather haphazard arrangement,\" I said. \"What time did you have lunch on the day of the murder?\" [2744] \"That was at twelve. You know, Ingrid was going out. She was going to see a movie or get her hair cut, and Mrs. Perry came to keep me company. She's really annoying, you know. She likes to pat people.\" [2745] \"Pat people?\" I was surprised. [2746] \"You know, in the head. Saying things like 'Dear little girl'.\" Geraldine said, \"She's not the type to chat with. But she'll bring me candies or things like that.\" [2747] \"How old are you, Geraldine?\" [2748] \"I am ten. Ten years and three months.\" [2749] \"You are very good at chatting, very smart,\" I said. [2750] \"That's because I have to talk to my dad all the time,\" Geraldine said seriously. [2751] \"So you had an early dinner on the day of the murder?\" [2752] \"Yes, so Ingrid could finish washing the dishes and leave by one.\" [2753] \"And then that morning you looked out the window at the passersby.\" [2754] Ingrid shook her head. [2755] \"No, I didn't. I find it strange.\" [2756] \"Hmm, maybe he came earlier.\" [2757] \"He didn't come to the front door, didn't ring the bell. Otherwise, I would have seen him.\" [2758] \"Perhaps he came through the garden. I mean, from the other side of the house, through the door.\" [2759] \"Oh, no,\" said Geraldine, \"the garden is facing away from the other house. Nobody likes it when someone just wanders through their own garden.\" [2760] \"Yes, I think so.\" [2761] \"I wish I knew what he looked like,\" said Geraldine. [2762] \"Well, he looked quite old. About sixty. His beard was neatly trimmed, and he was wearing a dark grey suit.\" [2763] Geraldine shook her head. [2764] \"That sounds very ordinary,\" she said, a little disappointed. [2765] \"Anyway,\" I said, \"I just want to say, it must have been quite a strain for you, looking out here every moment you were leaning on this wall.\" [2766] \"It wasn't difficult at all,\" she retorted. [2767] \"I can tell you everything that happened that morning. I know when Mrs. Crab came and when she left.\" [2768] \"The woman who cleans the house, is that it?\" [2769] \"Yes. She rushes around like a crab. She has a little boy. Sometimes she brings him with her, but not that day. Then Miss Pembroke went out around ten o'clock. She teaches at a school for the blind. Mrs. Crab left around noon. Sometimes she leaves with a package, but she didn't come with one that day. I think it was some butter and cheese for Miss Pembroke, because she can't see. I know a lot about what happened that day because, you know, I argued with Ingrid, so she wouldn't talk to me. I was teaching her English, and she wanted to know how to say 'until we meet again.' She only said it in German to me. I could understand it because when I went to Switzerland, people there said it to me. They said it too. If you say it in English, it sounds rude.\" [2770] \"So what did you say to Ingrid?\" [2771] Geraldine chuckled maliciously. She was about to speak when she broke into laughter again, but finally she managed to stop. [2772] \"I told her to say 'get out fast!' So she told it to Mrs. Bostrode next door, and she was furious. Ingrid got mad at me and said we weren't friends anymore until the next afternoon tea time when we made up.\" [2773] I pondered over what she said. [2774] \"So your attention has been on this telescope all this time.\" [2775] Geraldine nodded. [2776] \"That's why I know Mr. Corry didn't come in through the front door. I thought perhaps he managed to get in during the night and hid up in the attic. Do you think that's possible?\" [2777] \"I think anything can happen,\" I said, \"but in this case, it's unlikely.\" [2778] \"It's impossible,\" Geraldine said. \"He would have been starving if he hid. He wouldn't have been able to get breakfast from Miss Pembroke if he was hidden.\" [2779] \"Did no one come to the house?\" I asked. \"Not one person? No one came in a car? No business visitors?\" [2780] \"The grocer comes on Monday and Thursday,\" Geraldine said. \"The milkman comes every morning at eight-thirty.\" [2781] This child is like an encyclopedia. [2782] \"Cauliflower and other things are all bought by Miss Pemshaw herself. No one else has come to visit except the laundry people. It's a new laundry. She added.\" [2783] \"A new laundry?\" [2784] \"Yes. Usually it's Southdowns Laundry. Many people's clothes are sent to Southdowns Laundry. That day, it was a new laundry - Snowflake Laundry. I've never seen Snowflake Laundry before. It must be newly opened.\" [2785] I tried to control my voice, not to sound too excited. I didn't want to agitate her and make her too excited. [2786] \"Are they here to deliver clothes or collect them?\" I asked. [2787] \"To deliver clothes,\" said Geraldine. \"In a very large basket. Much larger than an ordinary basket.\" [2788] \"Did Miss Pemmican bring it herself?\" [2789] \"No, of course