 she picked up the fruit knife. \"It's not mine,\" she said wistfully. \"I wish it was.\" [2809] \"It seems like it doesn't belong to anyone, right?\" [2810] \"So, is it a lost treasure or something?\" [2811] \"Something like that,\" I say. \"I think you'd better hold on to it. That is, hold on tight until someone claims it's theirs. But I don't think they will.\" I say it sincerely. [2812] \"Give me an apple, Ingrid,\" Geraldine says. [2813] \"An apple? An apple [1]?\" [2814] Her pronunciation is good. I leave the knife and walk away. [2815] * * *\n[2816][1] The original was in French and German. [2817] Chapter 26\n[2818] Mrs. Rivale pushed open the \"Pavilion of the Peacock\" door and wobbled inside. She murmured. She was no stranger to this particular bar and was well-liked by the bartender. [2819] \"How are you, Flo?\" he said. \"What's wrong?\" [2820] \"That's not right,\" Mrs. Rivale said. \"It's not fair. No, it's not. I know what I'm saying, Fried, I say, that's not right.\" [2821] \"Of course, that's not right,\" Fried consoled her. \"What's the matter, dear? As usual?\" [2822] Madame Rival nodded her consent. She ordered a drink and began sipping from her glass. Fried went to attend to another customer. Under the influence of alcohol, Madame Rival gradually became more cheerful. She continued to mutter to herself, but her demeanor was noticeably more pleasant. When Fried came back, she spoke to him in a much gentler tone. [2823] \"Anyway, I'm not going to put up with it anymore,\" she said. \"No, no. If there's one thing I can't tolerate, it's deception. I can't stand it anymore. I just can't.\" [2824] \"Of course,\" said Fried. [2825] He looked at her with piercing eyes. \"It's happened several times,\" he thought to himself. \"I thought she could take more. Something must have upset her.\" [2826] \"Deception,\" Mrs. Rival said. \"Evasion, evasion, you know what I mean.\" [2827] \"I certainly do,\" Friederich said. [2828] He turned to greet the new guests