e could remember the man in the photo. [1125] He sighed. [1126] \"I think he was driving a big truck,\" said Mrs. McNutt, \"but I can't remember when I saw him. I think it was a bakery truck.\" [1127] \"Did you see him yesterday, Mrs. McNutt?\" [1128] Mrs. McNutt's face fell. She brushed her greasy gray curls back from her forehead. [1129] \"No, no, not yesterday,\" she said, \"at least -\" She stopped, \"I think not.\" Then her face brightened again. \"Maybe my husband will remember.\" [1130] \"Is he home?\" [1131] \"Oh, he's out in the garden,\" she said, pointing out the window. An older man was pushing a wheelbarrow along a path. [1132] \"Perhaps we should go out and talk to him.\" [1133] \"Of course, come this way.\" [1134] She led them through a side door and into the garden. Mr. MacNorton looked flushed and sweaty. [1135] \"These two gentlemen are from the police station, Angus,\" his wife panted. \"They want to ask about the murder at Miss Pemmican's house. Here's a picture of the deceased. Do you know, I'm sure I've seen him before. Was it last week when he came asking if we had any antiques to dispose of? Was it that man?\" [1136] \"Let me see,\" Mr. McNaughton said, \"just hold it up for me. I've got mud on my hands and can't touch anything.\" He addressed Mr. Harkness, \"You know about my gardening, don't you?\" [1137] \"Yes, Mr. Harkness said so,\" Mr. McNaughton grunted. [1138] \"Not Mrs. Lymes, I hope.\" [1139] \"No, it was Mr. Brand.\" [1140] Angus McNaughton snorted. [1141] \"Brand doesn't know what gardening is,\" he said, \"he just digs holes and then plants begonias, geraniums, and Cape tulips. That's not what I call gardening. Those flowers would survive in the park. Are you interested in shrubs, Inspector? Of course, it's not the season for planting shrubs, but I have a couple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[453] \"Alright, alright,\" Dick said. \"You win.\" [454] Chapter 5\n[455] \"Mom!\" O'Neil Courtin stopped and said, sliding a small metal rocket model up and down the windowpane to simulate the rocket's journey through space and into the orbit of Venus, \"Mom, what are you thinking?\" [456] Mrs. Cortin, a stern-faced woman, was busy washing ceramic dishes in the sink and didn't respond. [457] \"Mom, a police car is parked outside our house.\" [458] \"Didn't I tell you not to lie anymore, O'Neil?\" Mrs. Cortin said, roughly placing the dishes on the drying rack. \"Do you remember what I told you before?\" [459] \"I'm not lying,\" O'Neil insisted, \"there really is a police car, and two men got out of it.\" [460] Mrs. Cortin suddenly turned to her child. [461] \"What have you done?\" she demanded. \"Bringing shame and disgrace upon us? That's what you've done!\" [462] \"Of course not,\" Oney said. \"I didn't do anything.\" [463] \"It's those Alf and his gang,\" Mrs. Korting said. \"He and his cronies. A bunch of thugs! I told you, your father told you, they're no good. They always get into trouble. First, they'll be brought before the juvenile court, then sent to a juvenile detention center. I don't want you to turn out like that, do you hear?\" [464] \"They're at the front door,\" Oney said loudly. [465] Mrs. Korting put down her work in the sink and walked over to the window next to her child. [466] \"Hmm,\" she grumbled. [467] Just then, there was a knock at the door. Mrs. Cording quickly wiped her hands with a tea towel and went through the hallway to open the door. She looked at the two men on the doorstep with contempt and suspicion. [468] \"Are you Mrs. Cording?\" the taller man asked politely. [469] \"Yes,\" said Mrs. Cording. [470] \"Can I come in? I'm Detective Hakaste.\" [471] Mrs. Corton reluctantly moved back. She opened the door and gestured for the detective to come in. The room was spotlessly clean, giving the impression that few visitors were welcomed, and this was indeed the case. [472] Enoch, curious, came out of the kitchen and quietly leaned against the door. [473] \"Your son?\" Detective Inspector Hakast asked. [474] \"Yes,\" Mrs. Corton said, defending him with a defiant tone, \"He's a good boy, not what you think.\" [475] \"I believe him to be,\" Detective Inspector Hakast said politely. [476] Mrs. Cottington's hostility subsided. [477] \"I came to ask you a few questions about 19 Wilbraham Crescent. I understand you work there.\" [478] \"I haven't denied that,\" Mrs. Cottington said, still not completely recovered from her previous emotions. [479] \"You work for Miss Millechamp.\" [480] \"Yes, Miss Millechamp is a very kind lady.\" [481] \"Blind,\" said Inspector Hakast. [482] \"Yes, poor thing. But perhaps you didn't know, she has a pair of magic hands, and she can easily cope with life through touch. She can even go out on the street and cross the road by herself. She's different from some people I know who are always whining about nothing.\" [483] \"Do you usually work there in the morning?\" [484] \"Yes. I get there around nine-thirty to ten, and leave at twelve, or whenever I finish my work.\" [485] \"On the contrary,\" thought the inspector, thinking of the four clocks. [486] Mrs. Corten looked at him puzzled. [487] \"What happened?\" she asked. [488] \"A man was found dead in the living room of 19 Wilbraham Crescent this afternoon,\" the detective said. [489] Mrs. Corten's eyes widened. Eunice Corten twisted and turned in excitement, saying \"Oh\" with her mouth open, but then closed it again when she realized someone might see her. [490] \"Dead?\" Mrs. Corten couldn't believe it. And even more puzzling was, \"In the living room?\" [491] \"Yes, he was stabbed,\" he said. [492] \"You mean someone killed him?\" she asked. [493] \"Yes, he was killed,\" he replied. [494] \"Who did it?\" Mrs. Courting pressed. [495] \"That's all we know so far,\" said Huckleberry. \"We thought maybe you could help us.\" [496] \"I had nothing to do with the murder,\" Mrs. Kittredge said firmly. [497] \"Not about the murder, but there are one or two things we'd like to ask you. Did anyone come to see you this morning?\" [498] \"I don't remember anyone. No, not today. Who was he?\" [499] \"He was about sixty, wearing a well-dressed dark suit. He may have claimed to be an insurance agent for a company.\" [500] \"I wouldn't let him in,\" Mrs. Kittredge said. \"There's no insurance agent, and no one selling vacuum cleaners or encyclopedias. Pembroke wouldn't tolerate them, and neither would I.\" [501] \"According to the information on the card he brought, his name is Mr. Corley. Have you ever heard of that name?\" [502] \"Corley? Corley?\" Mrs. Cortlandt shook her head. \"Sounds like an Indian name.\u201d She guessed. [503] \"Oh, no,\" Hercule said, \"he's not Indian.\" [504] \"Who found him, Miss Pemerton?\" [505] \"A young lady, a stenographer, who came here by mistake, thinking she was sent to work for Miss Pemerton. She found the body. Miss Pemerton came home almost at the same time.\" [506] Mrs. Huttington sighed deeply. [507] \"How could this happen,\" she said, \"how could this happen!\" [508] \"We would like to ask you to look at the body of this man, at your convenience, and tell us if you have seen him on Wilbraham Crescent or if he ever visited Miss Pemarhu. Miss Pemarhu is quite certain he never came to her house. There are a few points I would like to ask you about, can you remember how many clocks there were in her sitting room?\" [509] Mrs. Huttington hesitated not at all. [510] \"The big clock was in the corner, they called it a grandfather clock; and there was a cuckoo clock on the wall, with a bird that would pop out and say 'cuckoo.' It didn't startle you, did it?\" She added quickly, \"I never touched any of them, never. Miss Pemarhu liked to wind them herself.\" [511] \"Those two clocks are all right,\" said the detective, reassuring her. \"Are you sure there were only those two in the room this morning?\" [512] \"Of course. Should there have been more?\" [513] \"For example, was there a square silver clock, a traveling clock? Or a gilded little clock on a shelf, or a ceramic clock with a patterned face, or a leather clock with the word 'Rosemary' written on one side?\" [514] \"Certainly not. I have never seen any of those things.\" [515] \"If they were there, you would have noticed them, wouldn't you?\" [516] \"Certainly.\" [517] \"These four clocks are all faster than the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock by an hour and a half.\" [518] \"Maybe they're foreign clocks,\" Mrs. Cochran said. \"My husband and I went on a trip to Switzerland and Italy, and their time was