ng space was immediately overshadowed by the exciting conversation of the day. [536] \"Miss Pemaroo couldn't have done that, could she?\" he ventured to analyze. [537] \"Don't talk nonsense,\" said his mother. An idea flashed through her mind. \"I don't know whether I should tell him\u2014\"\n[538] \"Tell him what, mom?\" [539] \"It's none of your business,\" said Mrs. Kettlety. \"In fact, there's nothing to tell.\" [540] Chapter Six\n[541] Colin Lumb's Narrative\n[542] 1\n[543] After having two half-cooked steaks and a pint of beer outside, Dick Hakaste satisfiedly sighed and announced that he felt better, then said,\n[544] \"Dead insurance agents, strange clocks, and screaming girls, go to hell! Let's talk about you, Colin. I thought you disappeared from the world. I didn't expect to see you wandering around Croton's back street. I can assure you that marine biologists won't find their calling in Croton.\" [545] \"Do you look down on marine biology, Dick? It's a useful profession. But it's so boring to talk about that people get annoyed when you mention it, so you don't need to explain further.\" [546] \"No chance to show yourself off, eh?\" [547] \"You forget,\" I said coldly, \"I'm an oceanographer. I have a degree in the subject from Cambridge University. Not very good, but at least it's a degree. It's an interesting profession, and someday I'll go back to it.\" [548] \"Of course, I know what you've been doing,\" Hackerst said. \"Congratulations. The trial of Rackin is next month, isn't it?\" [549] \"Yes.\" [550] \"It's amazing how he managed to smuggle the information out. I thought someone would suspect him.\" [551] \"No one would suspect him,\" I said. [552] \"He must be smart,\" Dick commented. [553] I shook my head. [554] \"No, I don't think he's smart. In fact, I think he just did what he was told to do. He could get his hands on very important documents. He took them out with him, had them photographed, gave them back, and then they were sent back to their original location the same day. Everything was arranged in a very tight and compact manner. He developed a habit of having lunch in different places. We thought he always hung his coat in the same place, but it was actually a coat identical to his, and the man wearing it was never the same person. The coat was switched, but the man who switched it never spoke to Rake, and Rake never spoke to him. We wanted to know more technical details. This plan was flawless, and the timing was precise. This person is indeed very smart.\" [555] \"That's why you're still hanging around the Portsmouth Naval Base, right?\" [556] \"Yes, we know the location in the naval base and the location in London. We know when and where Rackin collects his salary and how he collects it. But there's a gap between these two points. There's a subtle and delicate organization between them. That's where we want to know more, because that's where the brainpower comes into play. There's a great command center here, a route that will make you doubtful not once, but seven or eight times. It's a superb plan.\" [557] \"Why did Rackin do all this?\" Hakast asked curiously, \"For political ideals? Promoting individualism? Or for quick money?\" [558] \"He's not an idealist,\" I said. \"I think it's 'just for money'.\" [559] \"If you had started from that angle earlier, wouldn't you have found him faster? He must have spent the money. He doesn't save money.\" [560] \"Oh, that's right! He spent all the money. In fact, I was a bit hasty in dealing with him.\" [561] Hakast nodded understandingly. [562] \"I see. You fell once and then gradually used him. Right?\" [563] \"More or less. Before we found him, he had already taken out some valuable intelligence, so we let him leak more intelligence, obviously very valuable. In my workplace, we sometimes have to act like idiots.\" [564] \"I don't like your job, Colin,\" Hakast said thoughtfully. [565] \"It's not the kind of exciting job people think it is,\" I said. \"In fact, it's usually monotonous, but not just boring. Now people have a feeling that there is nothing that is truly secret. We know their secrets, and they know ours. Our agents are often their agents, and their agents are often ours. In the end, whoever is the double agent, it becomes a nightmare! Sometimes, I think, everyone knows each other's secrets, but they set up conspiracies and pretend not to know.\" [566] \"I see what you mean,\" Dick said kindly. [567] He looked at me curiously. [568] \"I know why you're still in Potterbury. But Croton is ten miles away from Potterbury.\" [569] \"Actually, my real purpose is,\" I said, \"New Moon Street.\" [570] \"New Moon Street?\" Hakast looked puzzled. [571] \"Yes. Or, the moon, the new moon, the rising moon, etc. I started my exploration from Portbury. There's a pub called 'New Moon' there. I spent a lot of time there. It sounds a bit crazy. Then there's 'Moon and Stars', 'Rising Moon', 'Happy Scythe', and 'Cross and Crescent' \u2013 that one's in a place called Simde. Nothing came of it. So I gave up on the moon pubs and started studying crescent-shaped streets. There are a few of them in Portbury. There's Lancelbury Crescent, Addington Crescent, Lifemide Crescent, and Victoria Crescent.\" [572] Suddenly, I saw a confused expression on Dick's face and started laughing. [573] \"Don't look so bewildered, Dick. There's something real waiting for me to start.\" [574] I took out my wallet and pulled out a piece of paper from it, handing it to him. It was a hotel stationery paper, on which a sketch was drawn. [575] \"This is something a boy named Hambley had in his wallet. He put a lot of effort into the case of Racking. He's a good guy, really good. He was hit by a car in London and died. The driver fled. No one saw the license plate. I don't know what this is, but it's something Hambley hastily wrote down or copied, thinking it was important. It made him think of something? Did he see or hear anything? Maybe something related to the moon or the new moon, and the number 61 and the capital letter M. After his death, this note fell into my hands. I don't know what I'm looking for, but I'm sure I'll find something. I don't know what 61 represents, or what M means. I've been busy for three weeks now, within a radius centered on Portbury. I haven't found anything. Cromwell is within my plans. That's it. To be honest, Dick, I don't have much hope for Cromwell. There's only one New Moon Street there, which is Wilbraham New Moon Street. Before asking you if you found any information that can help me, I'm going to walk along Wilbraham New Moon Street first and see what the number 61 looks like. That's what I did this afternoon, but I couldn't find number 61.\" [576] \"I told you, 61 is occupied by a local architect.\" [577] \"That's not what I'm looking for. Are there any foreigners living there?\" [578] \"There might be. Many people do these days. If there are, they'll be listed. I'll check for you tomorrow.\" [579] \"Thank you, Dick.\" [580] \"I'll be doing routine inquiries to the neighbors on either side of 19 tomorrow. Asking if they've seen anyone go to that house, and so on. I'll go to the house behind 19, the one whose garden connects with 19's. I seem to remember that 61 is right behind 19. If you'd like, I can take you along.\" [581] I eagerly agreed. [582] \"I'll be your RAMS officer, in charge of taking notes.\" [583] We agreed to meet at the police station at 9:30 the next morning. [584] 2\n[585] On the second day, I arrived at the appointed time and found my friend seething with anger. [586] After he sent away his sulking subordinate, I cautiously asked him what had happened. [587] For a moment, Hakaste seemed unable to speak. After a while, he blurted out, \"It's those damn clocks again!\" [588] \"Clocks again? What's the matter this time?\" [589] \"One of them is missing.\" [590] \"Missing? Which one?\" [591] \"The leather travel clock. The one with 'Rosemary' written on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nPerhaps Inna had a reason to know, or suspected that Miss Waite had impersonated Miss Pembroke's voice, calling to arrange for a stenographer to go to number 19? [1855] More conjecture. [1856] Or, of course, Sheila Webb herself...\n[1857] Detective Hardcastle's hand reached for the phone. He dialed the hotel where Colin Lumb was staying. He soon heard Colin's voice. [1858] \"This is Hardcastle. At what time did you and Sheila Webb have lunch today?\" [1859] There was a silence on the other end of the phone, then,\n[1860] \"How did you know we had lunch together?\" [1861] \"Just a guess. You were together, weren't you?\" [1862] \"Why shouldn't I have lunch with her?\" [1863] \"No reason. I just wanted to know the time. Did you go straight to lunch after the interrogation?\" [1864] \"No. She went shopping first. We met at the Chinese restaurant on Market Street at one o'clock.\" [1865] \"I see.\" [1866] Hakaste glanced down at his notepad. Iona Brent died between 12:30 and 1:00. [1867] \"You don't want to know what we had for lunch?\" [1868] \"Stay calm. I just want to know the exact time. For the record.\" [1869] \"I see. I hope so.\" [1870] Another silence ensued. Hakaste tried to lighten the mood\u2014\n[1871] \"If you don't have anything planned for tonight\u2014\"\n[1872] The other side interrupted him. [1873] \"I'm leaving. Packing up. I saw the message and need to go abroad.\" [1874] \"Who knows? At least a week, maybe longer, or maybe never!\" [1875] \"That's terrible, isn't it?\" [1876] Chapter 18\n[1877] 1\n[1878] When Hercule Poirot arrived at 19 Wilbraham Crescent, Miss Pembridge was just about to leave. [1879] \"I'm sorry to keep you, Miss Pembridge.\" [1880] \"Oh. You're \u2013 Inspector Hercule Poirot?\" [1881] \"Yes. Can I talk to you?\" [1882] \"I don't want to be late for school. Will it take long?\" [1883] She went inside, and he followed her. [1884] \"Did you hear about what happened this afternoon?\" he said. [1885] \"What happened?\" [1886] \"I thought you would have heard. A girl was killed in the phone booth down this way.\" [1887] \"Murdered? When?\" [1888] \"Two hours and forty-five minutes ago.\" He looked at the grandfather clock. [1889] \"I haven't heard anything about it, not at all.\" Miss Pemukh said, her voice suddenly sounding angry. It seemed that the girl's misfortune had touched her heart. \"A girl, murdered! What kind of girl?\" [1890] \"Her name is Ina Brent, she works at Cavendish Document Printing.\" [1891] \"Another girl from there! Was she also arranged to come here, her name is Sheila something?\" [1892] \"I don't think so,\" the detective said. \"Did she come here to see you?\" [1893] \"Come here? No, of course not.\" [1894] \"If she had come here, were you there?\" [1895] \"I'm not sure. What time are you talking about?\" [1896] \"Around 12:30 or a little later.\" [1897] \"Yes,\" said Miss Pemukho, \"I was at home then.\" [1898] \"Where did you go after the trial?\" [1899] \"I came straight back here.\" She paused and then asked, \"Why do you think this girl might have come to see me?\" [1900] \"Well, she was at the hearing this morning. She saw you. There must be a reason she came to Wilbraham Crescent. As far as we know, she doesn't know anyone on this street.\" [1901] \"But why did she come to find me just because she saw me in the interrogation room?\" [1902] \"Hmm...,\" the detective smiled slightly, quickly covering up the smile as he realized Miss Pema didn't like his attempts to get close to her. \"No one will ever know the reason now. Perhaps she just wanted a signature, or something similar.\" [1903] \"A signature!\" Miss Pemukh's voice was filled with contempt, and then she said, \"Yes... yes, I suppose you're right. Such things do happen.\u201d Then she shook her head briskly. \"But I can assure you, Inspector Hakaste, that such a thing has not happened today, and since I've been back from the interrogation, no one has come here.\u201d\n[1904] \"Well, thank you, Miss Pema. We thought it best to check every possibility.\" [1905] \"How old is she?\" Miss Pemaux asked. [1906] \"I believe she's nineteen years old.\" [1907] \"Nineteen? So young. Very young... poor child. Who could want to harm such a young girl?\" [1908] \"But it did happen,\" Detective Hardcastle said. [1909] \"Was she pretty... charming... seductive?\" [1910] \"No,\" Detective Hardcastle said. \"I think she wished to be, but she wasn't.\" [1911] \"Then it wasn't for that reason,\" Miss Pembroke said, shaking her head again. \"I'm sorry, I'm sorry, Detective Hakast, I couldn't help you.\" [1912] He left, as before, once again impressed by Miss Pembroke's personality. [1913] 2\n[1914] Miss Wutherspoon was also at home. She was still the same, suddenly opening the door as if deliberately tempting someone to do something they shouldn't. [1915] \"Oh, it's you!\" she said. \"In fact, I've told your people everything I know.\" [1916] \"I'm sure you've answered all the questions asked of you,\" Detective Hakast said. \"But not all questions can be answered at once, you know. We wanted to know some details.\" [1917] \"I don't understand why,\" Miss Walters said, looking at him with a nearly critical eye, as if it were all his fault. \"Please come in, please come in. You can't stand on the doormat all day. Come in, sit down, and ask whatever you want to ask. Although, to be honest, some questions I don't know. As I said, I went out to make a phone call, and as soon as I opened the door of the phone booth, I found the girl. I've never been so scared in my life. I rushed out to find the police. After that, if you want to know, I came back here and drank an equivalent of a medicinal dose of brandy, medicinally.\" [1918] \"You're very wise, madam,\" Detective Hakast said. [1919] \"That's all there is to it,\" Miss Walters said. [1920] \"I'd like to ask if you're absolutely sure you've never seen the girl before?\" [1921] \"I may have seen her many times,\" said Miss Watkins, \"but I don't remember. I mean, she may have served me at Woolworth's, or sat next to me on the bus, or sold me a ticket at the cinema.\" [1922] \"She was a stenographer at Cavenish Typewriting Agency.\" [1923] \"I don't think I've ever had the opportunity to use a stenographer. Maybe she worked at my brother's 'Gatesford and Swinburne Agency.' Is that what you want to know?\" [1924] \"No, no,\" said Detective Hercaster, \"that has nothing to do with it. I just want to know if she came to see you before she was killed this morning.\" [1925] \"To see me? No, of course not. Why would she do that?\" [1926] \"Well, we don't know about that,\" Detective Hacker said, \"but someone said they saw her come to your front gate this morning. Did they get it wrong?\" [1927] \"Someone saw her come to my front gate? Nonsense.\" [1928] \"What?\" [1929] \"Well, I guess she might have slipped a flyer or something through the door crack. I saw a flyer at lunchtime, about a disarmament conference. I think. Things happen every day. I guess she probably came by, put something in the mailbox, but you can't blame me for that, can you?\" [1930] \"Of course not. Now, let's talk about your phone. You said it was broken. The phone exchange says there's no such thing.\" [1931] \"The telephone exchange can say anything! When I dialed, there was a strange buzzing noise, not a busy signal, so I went to the phone booth.\" [1932] Poirot stood up. [1933] \"I'm sorry to disturb you like this, Miss Whitehouse, but this girl coming to New Moon Street might really have been visiting someone and gone to a house not far from here.\" [1934] \"So you should ask along New Moon Street,\" said Miss Whitehouse. \"I think she's most likely to have gone to the one next door \u2013 I mean Miss Pemarue.\" [1935] \"Why do you think that's the most likely?\" [1936] \"You said she was a stenographer from Cavendish Typewriting Agency. Of course, if I'm not mistaken, I heard just a few days ago, on the day that man was killed, Miss Pembury had a stenographer come to her.\" [1937] \"She denied it.\" [1938] \"Well, if you ask me,\" Miss Wattershouse said, \"I would say she might be a little odd - I mean Miss Pembury. I think, perhaps, she might have called the typing agency and asked for a stenographer to come to her. Then, perhaps, she forgot all about it.\" [1939] \"Do you think she could be the murderer?\" [1940] \"I never thought she'd go around killing someone or anything like that. I know someone was killed in her house, but I never suggested that had anything to do with Miss Pembury. No. I was just thinking she might have some odd preferences, like some other people. I know a woman who keeps calling the candy store and ordering a dozen sugar cookies. But she really doesn't need them, and when they're delivered, she says she didn't order them. Just that kind of thing.\" [1941] \"Of course, anything's possible.\" Hercast said. He left Miss Warton after bidding her goodbye. [1942] He thought she was just trying to clear herself of suspicion. In other words, if she believed someone saw the girl go into her house, then suggesting that the girl went into No. 19 becomes a clever way of covering up in this particular case. [1943] Hercast glanced at his watch and decided he had time to visit the Cavenish Document Printing Office. He knew they would be back at work at two o'clock after lunch. He might be able to get some help from the other girls there, and he could see Miss Sheila Webber. [1944] 3\n[1945] When he entered the office, one of the girls immediately stood up. [1946] \"Are you Inspector Hackett?\" she asked. \"Miss Martin is waiting for you.\" [1947] She led him into the inner office, where Miss Martin immediately began to berate him. [1948] \"This is simply disgraceful, Inspector Hackett, absolutely disgraceful! You must get to the bottom of this, you must get to the bottom of it immediately! Stop dilly-dallying! It's the police's duty to protect the public, and that's exactly what we need now. Protection. I want my girls to be protected, and we urgently need protection!\" [1949] \"Certainly, Miss Martin, that\u2014\"\n[1950] \"Are you going to deny that my two girls are victims? It's obvious, someone irresponsible, with some kind of, well, what they call these days, a perverted psychology, targets shorthand typists or typewriting agencies. They deliberately torture them. First, Miss Webber was cruelly teased, sent to discover a corpse, something that might drive a nervous girl insane. Now this has happened. A girl so innocent and kind was murdered in a telephone booth. You must investigate thoroughly, Inspector.\" [1951] \"What I want to do most now is to get to the bottom of this, Miss Martindeer. I came to see if you could give me some help.\" [1952] \"Help! What help can I give you? If I could help, I would have found you by now! You must find out who killed this poor girl, Iena, and who played this cruel trick on Miss Hila. I have high standards for my girls, Inspector, I expect them to be diligent and not be late or disheveled. But I cannot tolerate them being killed or involved in murder. I want to protect them, and I want to see the people paid by the state protect them so they can work in peace. She glared at him, looking like a furious tigress. [1953] \"Give us some time, Miss Martindeer.\" he said. [1954] \"Time? I guess you think you have all the time in the world because a silly girl died. The next one to be murdered will be another of these girls.\" [1955] \"I think you don't need to worry about that, Miss Martindeer.\" [1956] \"I suppose you didn't think this girl would be killed this morning, did you, Detective? If you had, you would have taken preventive measures to protect her. You would have been just as surprised when one of my girls was killed or found in a terrible compromising situation. The whole thing is so unusual, incredible! You must admit it's a mad scheme. That means, maybe what you read in the papers is true. Like those clocks. I noticed that nobody mentioned them at the trial this morning.\" [1957] \"We had to keep the questioning to a minimum this morning, Miss Martindale. It was just a postponement of the trial, you know.\" [1958] \"All I want to say is, Miss Martindale, that you have to take action on this.\" [1959] \"Didn't Ina give you any hints? Didn't she show any concern about something? Didn't she talk to you about it?\" [1960] \"I don't think she would have talked to me even if she was worried,\" said Miss Martindale. \"But what was she worried about?\" [1961] This was exactly what Inspector Hercule Poirot wanted to know. He knew he wouldn't get an answer from Miss Martin. So he said:\n[1962] \"I want to talk to each girl here. I understand Ina Brent wouldn't confide her fears or worries to you, but she might have confided in her colleagues.\" [1963] \"That's probably true,\" Miss Martin said. \"They're always gossiping - these girls. As soon as they hear my footsteps in the corridor, all the typewriters start working. But what were they doing before that? Chattering, chattering, never stopping! \"After a moment's pause, she said, \"There are only three girls in the office now. Do you want to talk to them now? The others are out on business. I can give you their names and addresses if you need them.\" [1964] \"Thank you, Miss Martin.\" [1965] \"I hope you talk to them alone,\" Miss Martin said. \"If I stand here and watch, they'll feel uncomfortable. Because, you understand, then they'll have to admit they've been gossiping and wasting time.\" [1966] She rose from her chair and opened the door, entering the outer office. [1967] \"Girls,\" she said, \"Inspector Hakast wants to talk to you. You stop what you're doing. Talk to the inspector properly and see if you can help him find the killer of Ina Brent.\u201d\n[1968] She returned to her office and slammed the door. Three girls, looking frightened, stared at the inspector. One was blonde, wearing glasses, and looked trustworthy but not very intelligent. One looked mischievous, with dark skin and hair, her hair disheveled as if she had just been through a storm. Her eyes darted around the room, and he suspected that what she said might not be entirely truthful. Everything had to be handled carefully. [1969] He spoke calmly and casually. [1970] \"Have you all heard about what happened to your colleague Ina Brent?\u201d\n[1971] Three heads nodded vigorously. [1972] \"By the way, how did you find out?\" [1973] They looked at each other, deciding who should speak first. After unanimous agreement, the blonde girl spoke. Her name was apparently Janet. [1974] \"Ina didn't come back to work at two o'clock as usual,\" she explained. [1975] \"The cat was furious,\" said the black-haired girl, Melvin, before correcting herself, \"I mean, Miss Martin.\" [1976] The third girl chuckled. \"The cat is our nickname for her,\" she explained. [1977] \"Not a bad name,\" the detective thought. [1978] \"She can be quite terrifying when she's angry,\" Melanie said, \"She'll scold you fiercely. She asked us if Ina had said she wouldn't come to the office today and said she should at least give a reason.\" [1979] The blonde girl said, \"I told Miss Martinfield that she went with us to the courthouse, but we didn't see her after that, and we don't know where she went.\" [1980] \"That's true, isn't it?\" Hakaste asked, \"You don't know where she went after leaving the courthouse.\" [1981] \"I suggested we all go to lunch,\" Morin said. \"But she seemed preoccupied. She said she wasn't sure if she should eat lunch or buy something to eat in the office.\" [1982] \"So, she meant she'd come back to the office after that?\" [1983] \"Oh, yes, of course. We all have to come back to work.\" [1984] \"Has anyone noticed anything different about Ina Brent these past few days? Does she seem troubled, like she's thinking about something? Has she told you what's bothering her? If you know anything, please tell me.\" [1985] They looked at each other, their exchange lacking any attempt at concealment. It seemed to be merely vague speculation. [1986] \"She's often been in a state of mind, \" said Molly. \"She's been making mistakes and getting things mixed up. Her reactions are always a beat behind.\" [1987] \"It seems like there's always some kind of trouble with Iona,\" said the smiling girl. \"Remember when her high heel fell off a few days ago? Things like that always happen to Iona.\" [1988] \"I remember,\" said Hakast.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] Chapter 1\n[2] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[3] 1\n[4] According to police records: At 2:59 PM on September 9th, I was walking west on Wilbraham Crescent. This was my first time here, and I must admit, everything about the place left me bewildered. [5] For some time now, I've had a feeling, and this feeling has been growing stronger and stronger, almost impossible to shake off. That's just how I felt. [6] I was looking for a house numbered 61, and I thought, maybe I won't find it. I had carefully found houses from 1 to 35, and just then, I discovered that Wilbraham Crescent had reached its end. A road called Albany Avenue rudely blocked my path. I turned around. There were no houses to the north, only a wall. Behind this wall, there were towering modern apartments, and it was clear that the entrance was on another road. It was impossible to find a way from here. [7] As I walked, I paid attention to the house numbers I passed by \u2013 24, 23, 22, 21, Diana's Cottage (which must be number 20, with an orange cat washing itself on the gatepost), and 19. [8] The door to number 19 suddenly opened, and a young girl rushed out, then ran up the path. I thought a missile's speed was nothing compared to hers. She ran with such a sharp cry that she looked even more like a missile. The sound was high-pitched and pitiful. The girl ran through the gate and collided with me, almost knocking me off the sidewalk. She not only collided with me but also grabbed me tightly, desperately clinging to me. [9] \"Take it easy,\" I said, gently shaking her when I regained my balance, \"Now take it easy.\" [10] The girl's emotions finally calmed down. Although she was still holding on to me tightly, she was no longer screaming, but only panting heavily, gasping and sobbing. [11] In this situation, I must say that my reaction was not quick enough. I asked her what happened. Realizing that my tone was too weak, I immediately changed it. [12] \"What happened?\" [13] The girl took a deep breath. [14] \"Over there!\" She pointed to the place behind her. [15] \"Then what?\" [16] \"There was a man lying on the floor... dead... she was about to step on him.\" [17] \"Who? How did this happen?\" [18] \"I think, because she's blind. And there was blood on him.\" [19] \"Indeed, there was blood.\" I said, looking at the stain on my sleeve. \"Now I'm the same.\" I pointed to the stain. Thinking about the current situation, I sighed. \"You should take me there to see.\" [20] But she began to shake her head vigorously. [21] \"I can't go, I can't go... I don't want to go back there.\" [22] \"Maybe you're right.\" I looked around. There didn't seem to be a place to put a girl who was about to faint. I gently guided her, letting her lean against the iron railing. [23] \"Stay here,\" I said, \"don't leave until I come back. I won't be long.\" I leaned forward and if you feel uncomfortable, put your head between your knees, it will feel better.\" [24] \"I, I think I'm okay now.\" [25] Although she said so, she was still a little shaken, but I didn't want to talk to her too much. I gently patted her shoulder, comforted her, and then walked quickly up the path. I went through the door into the house, hesitated slightly in the hallway, observed the left door, which was an empty restaurant, and then walked through the foyer into the opposite living room. [26] The first thing I saw was an old woman with gray hair sitting on a chair. When I went in, her head turned quickly towards me and said,\n[27] \"Who came?\" [28] I immediately realized that the woman's eyes were blind. Her eyes looked straight at me, then swept past me and landed on the back of my left ear. [29] I said directly:\n[30] \"A young girl ran up the street, saying that someone died here.\" [31] I felt that what I said was absurd. In this clean room, the woman sat with her hands folded on the chair, looking so calm. It seemed impossible to find a dead man here. [32] But she immediately answered. [33] \"Behind the sofa,\" she said. [34] I went around the corner of the sofa. Then I saw the open arms, the vacant eyes, and the congealed blood clots. [35] \"How did this happen?\" I suddenly asked. [36] \"I don't know.\" [37] \"So, who is he?\" [38] \"I don't know.\" [39] \"We have to call the police.\" I look around. \"Where's the phone?\" \"I don't have a phone.\" [40] I stare at her intently. [41] \"Are you living here? Is this your house?\" [42] \"Yes.\" [43] \"Can you tell me what happened?\" [44] \"Of course. I came back with my shopping -\" I noticed the shopping bag thrown on the chair near the door. \"I came home and immediately sensed that there was someone else in the house. A blind person is very sensitive to such things. I asked who was in the house. No one answered me. I only heard someone breathing heavily. I went towards the source of the sound. Then someone yelled out - someone had died, I was about to step on the body. Then someone screamed past me, running out of the room.\" [45] I nodded. They both told the same story. [46] \"What did you do then?\" [47] \"I walked carefully until my foot touched something.\" [48] \"What happened next?\" [49] \"I squatted down. I felt a man's hand. It was cold and there was no pulse\u2026 I got up and walked over here, sat down and waited for someone to come. It won't be long, someone will come. That young woman, whoever she is, will call for help. I think it's best not to leave this house.\" [50] I was shocked by the woman's calmness. She didn't scream or panic in the house, she just sat there quietly waiting. She was rational, but we still had to take action. [51] She questioned:\n[52] \"Who are you?\" [53] \"My name is Colin Lamb. I just happened to pass by.\" [54] \"Where did the young girl go?\" [55] \"I let her rest near the entrance. She was scared. Where is the nearest place to call for help?\" [56] \"Follow this path for about fifty yards, and there's a phone booth just before the corner.\" [57] \"Yes, I remember passing by there. I'm going to call the police. You\u2014\" I hesitated. [58] I didn't know whether to say \"Are you going to stay here?\" or \"Are you okay?\" [59] She spared me the decision. [60] \"You should bring the girl inside,\" she said firmly. [61] \"I don't know if she'll come,\" I said skeptically. [62] \"Of course, don't let her come into this room. Tell her to wait in the dining room next to the entrance. Tell her I'm brewing tea for her.\" [63] She approached me. [64] \"But can you do that?\" she asked. [65] A slight smirk appeared on her face. [66] \"My dear young man, since I moved into this house fourteen years ago, I have been cooking in the kitchen myself. Being blind doesn't mean I'm useless.\" [67] \"I'm sorry, I was too ignorant. Perhaps I can ask for your name?\" [68] \"Millee Pema-Chhoe \u2013 Miss.\" [69] I left the room and walked out along the path. The girl saw me and struggled to get up. [70] \"I, I think I'm almost better now.\" [71] I helped her stand up, smiling,\n[72] \"That's good.\" [73] \"There, there, someone died, right?\" [74] I immediately nodded. [75] \"Indeed. I'm going to the phone booth to report the incident to the police. If I were you, I would wait in the house. Go to the restaurant and wait on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" I raised my voice to prevent her from immediately contradicting me. \"Wait in the restaurant, on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" [76] \"So that's Miss Pemakh, is it? Can she see?\" [77] \"Yes, she is. This has certainly shocked her, but she has been very rational about it. Come on, I'll take you in. Having a cup of tea while waiting for the police will make you feel better.\" [78] I put my arm around her shoulder and led her down the path. After settling her comfortably at the table, I rushed to the phone booth. [79] 2\n[80] A cold voice said, \"Croedin Police Station.\" [81] \"Is Inspector Hatchet there?\" [82] The voice suddenly became cautious:\n[83] \"I don't know. Who is this?\" [84] \"Tell him it's Colin Loom.\" [85] \"Please wait.\" [86] I waited. Then Dick Hakast's voice came over the line. [87] \"Is that Colin? It's been a while. Where are you?\" [88] \"Crofton. To be precise, I'm on the floor of 19 Wilbraham Crescent. There's a man lying there, dead, I think stabbed. Probably died about half an hour ago.\" [89] \"Who found him? You?\" [90] \"No, a stranger. A girl came running out of the house, almost knocking me over. She said there was a man lying on the floor of the house, dead. A blind woman almost stepped on his body.\" [91] \"You're not pulling my leg, are you?\" Dick asked skeptically. [92] \"I admit, it sounds incredible, but that's the truth. The woman is the owner of the house, Miss Mirella Pembleton.\" [93] \"The one who almost tripped over the dead man?\" [94] \"Not like you think. If she was blind, she wouldn't have noticed the dead man lying there.\" [95] \"I'll be right there. Wait for me there. How did you take care of the girl?\" [96] \"Miss Pemah is brewing tea for her.\" [97] Dic commented that the arrangement sounded somewhat soothing. [98] Chapter Two\n[99] At 19 Wilbraham Crescent, the police officers had arrived. A forensic expert, a photographer, and a fingerprint examiner were present. They worked in an orderly manner, with each person performing their designated task efficiently. [100] The last to arrive was Inspector Hakast, a tall man with expressive eyebrows and a stern expression. He observed that all the work he had arranged was proceeding smoothly. He glanced at the corpse, exchanged a few brief words with the forensic expert, and then went to the dining room. There, he found three people seated with three empty teacups in front of them. They were Miss Pemah, Colin Lum, and a tall girl with brown curls. The girl's eyes were wide with fear. \"Very nice,\" the inspector remarked in his usual witty manner. [101] He introduced himself to Miss Pema. [102] \"Detective Inspector Dick Hakast.\" [103] He knew something about Miss Pema, despite their lines of work never intersecting. He had seen her a few times before, knowing her to be a teacher at the Allenburg Institute, teaching blind script to disabled children. The discovery of a man dead of murder in such a clean, simple house seemed improbable. But often the most improbable things are the most common. [104] \"This is a truly awful thing, Miss Pema,\" he said, \"and I'm afraid it's quite a shock to you. I need to get a clear understanding of the whole incident from you. I know -\" he glanced quickly at the police report he had been given, \"Hilary Webb was the one who actually found the body. Miss Pema, if you allow me to use your kitchen, I would like to bring Miss Webb there and speak with her privately.\" [105] He opened the door connecting the dining room and the kitchen, waiting for the girl to enter. A young plainclothes detective was already in the kitchen, sitting at a table with a Formica-topped surface, writing something quietly. [106] \"This chair looks quite comfortable,\" Hakcastle said, pulling a modern-style Windsor chair. [107] Hilary Webber sat nervously, her big fearful eyes staring straight at him. [108] Hakcastle almost said, \"Dear, I won't eat you,\" but he held back and said instead,\n[109] \"Don't worry. We just want to know what happened. Your name is Hilary Webber. Your address?\" [110] \"14 Palmerston Road, a little further down the gas works.\" [111] \"Okay, next. I assume you have a job?\" [112] \"Yes, I'm a stenographer typist, and I work at Miss Martin's Document Printing Agency.\" [113] \"Cavendish Document Printing Agency, is that the full name?\" [114] \"Yes.\" [115] \"How long have you been working there?\" [116] \"About a year. Well, to be exact, ten months.\" [117] \"Okay. Now tell me, how did you end up at 19 Wilbraham Crescent today?\" [118] \"Well, it's like this. Miss Pember called the typewriting agency and asked for a stenographer to come here at 3 pm. So when I came back from lunch, Miss Martin sent me here.\" [119] \"Is that just a regular shift, or do you guys arrange these kinds of jobs differently?\" [120] \"Actually, it's not exactly like that. Miss Pember specifically asked for me to come.\" [121] \"Ms. Pemakh specifically asked for you to come.\" Mr. Hakast's eyebrows remained unmoving. \"I see, because you worked for her before?\" [122] \"But I didn't.\" Sheila immediately answered. [123] \"You didn't work for her before? Are you sure?\" [124] \"Yes, I'm positive. I mean, she's not the kind of person you forget. That's what's strange about it.\" [125] \"It is quite unusual. Well, let's not discuss that for now. When did you get here?\" [126] \"It must have been around three o'clock, because that cuckoo clock\u2014\" She immediately stopped. Her eyes widened. \"How strange. It's so strange. I didn't really pay attention to the time then.\" [127] \"Did you notice anything, Miss Webber?\" [128] \"How could I have not noticed those clocks?\" [129] \"What about those clocks?\" [130] \"The cuckoo clock struck three, but the others were almost an hour ahead. How strange!\" [131] \"That is strange,\" the detective replied. \"So, when was the first time you found the body?\" [132] \"It wasn't until I turned around the sofa that I noticed it. There he was, lying there. It was terrible, so terrible\u2026\"\n[133] \"Terrible! I agree. Do you know the man? Have you seen him before?\" [134] \"No, I haven't.\" [135] \"Are you sure you've never seen him? He might look different now than when you last saw him. Think carefully. Are you absolutely certain you've never seen him before?\" [136] \"Absolutely.\" [137] \"Alright. That's enough about that. So, what did you do next?\" [138] \"What did I do?\" [139] \"Yes.\" [140] \"Why are you asking? I didn't do anything, absolutely nothing. I couldn't have done anything.\" [141] \"I understand. You didn't touch him at all?\" [142] \"Oh, yes, I did. I touched him to see if... I mean, just to confirm, but he was so cold, and then, then my hand got covered in blood. It was so horrible, sticky.\" [143] She started shaking. [144] \"Okay, okay,\" Detective Hakast said in a soothing tone, \"Let's move on from that. Forget about the blood. What happened next?\" [145] \"I don't know... Yes, she came back then.\" [146] \"You mean Miss Pemakho?\" [147] \"Yes. I didn't realize it was Miss Pemakho. She just happened to come in with a shopping bag.\" [148] \"So, what did you say?\" [149] \"I think I didn't say anything... I tried to speak, but I couldn't make a sound. It felt like my throat was being squeezed.\" [150] The detective nodded. [151] \"Then, she said, 'Who's there?' and she came around the sofa, and I thought, I thought she was going to step on him. That's when I screamed... Once I started screaming, I couldn't stop. I don't know how I just ran out of the house and out the front door.\" [152] \"Like a ghost,\" the detective remembered Colin's description. [153] Shira Webber looked at him with eyes full of pain and fear, and unexpectedly said,\n[154] \"I'm sorry.\" [155] \"There's nothing to be sorry about. You did well. Don't think about it anymore. Oh, and one more thing, why were you in that room?\" [156] \"Why?\" She looked confused. [157] \"Yes. You may have arrived a few minutes early, I think, and then you rang the doorbell. But if no one answered, how did you get in?\" [158] \"Oh, that. She told me to come in.\" [159] \"Who told you?\" [160] \"Miss Pemaroo.\" [161] \"But I thought you hadn't spoken to her.\" [162] \"Yes, we hadn't. Miss Martindale told me to wait in the sitting room on the right in the hall.\" [163] Hakast pondered, \"So that's how it is!\" [164] Sheila Weber timidly asked:\n[165] \"And is there anything else?\" [166] \"I think so. I'd like you to stay here for about ten minutes, in case I think of anything else to ask you. Then, I'll send a police car to take you home. What's your family situation?\" [167] \"My parents are dead. I live with my aunt.\" [168] \"What's her name?\" [169] \"Mrs. Lott.\" [170] The detective rose and held out his hand. [171] \"Thank you very much, Miss Webber,\" he said. \"Have a good night's sleep and rest well. You need plenty of sleep after all this.\" [172] She timidly smiled at him and then entered the dining room.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."